1000:
** βολή**, ἡ,
**throw**:
**1** **stroke** or **wound** of a missile (opposed to πληγή, of sword or pike), {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.283, compare 24.161; β. πέτρων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 59; λίθων 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.31 W. (_plural_); μέχρι λίθου καὶ ἀκοντίου βολῆς 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.65; β. ἔρωτος}}**shafts of love,** {{Refs|Anthologia Graeca 12.160}}; βολαῖς.. σφόγγος ὤλεσεν γραφήν by its **stroke** or **touch**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1329}}; **swing** of ἁλτῆρες, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 6.34.1.}}
**2** κύβων βολαί **throws** or **casts** of dicc, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 429.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, β. ὀφθαλμῶν **quick glances**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.150; κάτω.. βλεμμάτων ῥέπει β. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 242, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vitae Sophistarum” 2.27.5.}}
**4** β. κεραύνιοι thunder-**bolts**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 430}}; βολαὶ ἡλίου sun-**beams**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 877, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1134}}; χρυσοῦ.. βολαῖς **with** golden **rays**, of a statue, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“IG” 14.1026 (3rd c.AD)}}; βολαὶ χιόνος **radiance**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 662; τὰς ψυχὰς οἷον βολὰς εἶναι λέγουσιν 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.4.3.}}
**5** βολαί, ={ὠδῖνες}, {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “de Bello Gothico” 4.22.}}
**6** **payment**, ἀποδώσω ἐπὶ βολαῖς δυσίν {{Refs 6th c.AD+|“Stud.Pal.” 20.139 (6th c.AD).}}

1002:
** βολίς**, ίδος, ἡ,
**missile, javelin**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Demetrius” 3.}}
**2** **sounding-lead**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.80.}}
**3** ἀστραπῶν βολίδες **flashes** of lightning, {{LXX|LXX.Wis.5.21}}; ἀστραπὴ βολίς (sic){{LXX|LXX.Zech.9.14.}}
**4** **cast of the dice,** {{Refs 6th c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 9.767 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus).}}
**4.b** **die**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 768 ([prev. author]).}}

1006:
** βόσκω**,
_imperfect_ ἔβοσκον, _Epic dialect_ βόσκε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.548}}: _future_ **-ήσω**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.559, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 590}}: _aorist_ ἐβόσκησα {{Refs|“Geoponica” 18.7}}: _perfect_ βεβόσκηκα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“PMag.” 6.13 (3rd c.BC):}} —_passive_ and _middle_ {{Refs|(see. below 11)}}; _Ionic dialect_ _imperfect_ βοσκέσκοντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.355:}}_future_ βοσκήσομαι Sarap. in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.398d}}, _Doric dialect_ βοσκησεῦμαι {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 5.103}}: _aorist_ ἐβοσκήθην {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 34, 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 89.7.}}
**I** properly of herdsmen, **feed, tend**, αἰπόλια {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.102; ταὦς 5th c.BC: Strattis Comicus 27}}; ὁ βόσκων **the feeder**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 540a18.}}
**I.2** generally, **feed, nourish**, βόσκει γαῖα.. ἀνθρώπους {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.365, compare 14.325; γαστέρα βοσκήσεις 17.559; πάντα βόσκουσαν φλόγα.. Ἡλίου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1425}}; **maintain, keep**, ἐπικούρους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.39; ναυτικόν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.48; γυναῖκας 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 260}}; οἰκέτας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 1204, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 7.44: _metaphorically_, β. νόσον 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 313}}; πράγματα β. troubles, i.e. children, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 313.}}
**II** _passive_, of cattle, **feed, graze**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.49, etc.; ξύλοχον κάτα 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.162}}: c._accusative_, **feed on**, ποίην {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“hymnus ad Mercurium” 27, 232, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 118 (Lyric poetry), 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 591a16, +others; τινί 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 244.}}
**II.2** _metaphorically_, **to be fed** or **nurtured**, ἰυγμοῖσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ch.” 26 (Lyric poetry); κούφοις πνεύμασιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 558; ἐλπίσιν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 617}}; β. τινί or περί τι **run riot in** a thing, {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 5.271 (Paul. Sil.), probably in 285 ([prev. author])}}. (**g[uglide]ō**, cf. Lithuanian _guotas_ 'herd'.)

1008:
** βοτάνη** [ᾰ], ἡ,
(βόσκω) **pasture**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.493, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 321b, etc.; ἐκ βοτάνης ἀνιόντα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 25.87; ἐν β. [prev. author] 28.12; ἔγρονται ἐς βοτάναν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 773.29}}; β. ἁ λέοντος the lion's **pasture**, i.e. Nemea, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.42: _metaphorically_, ὥσπερ ἐν κακῇ β. τρεφόμενοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 401c.}}
**2** **fodder**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.411.}}
**3** **herb**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.4.13, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1 “Praef.” 1 (_plural_), etc.}}
**4** in _plural_, **plants**, as material for making clothes, opposed to δοραί, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Diogenes Cynicus Philosophus Oen. 10.}}
**5** **weeds**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 2.7.5, “POxy.” 729.22 (2nd c.AD): in _plural_, “Geoponica” 2.46.2.}}
**6** ἱερὰ β., ={περιστερεών}, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 4.60.}}

1009:
** βότρυς**, υος, ὁ
(irregular _plural_ βότρυα, τά, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euphorio Epicus 149)}}, **bunch of grapes**, μέλανες δ᾽ ἀνὰ βότρυες ἦσαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.562, etc.}}: _plural_, **grapes**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 2.55: proverbial, βότρυς πρὸς βότρυν πεπαίνεται 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.225b.}}
**2** =[{{Refs 4th c.AD+|{βότρυχος} 1, βότρυς κόμης “Anthologia Graeca” 5.286 (6th c.AD: Agathias Historicus et Epigrammaticus), compare 4th-5th c.AD(?): Nonnus Epicus “Dionysiaca” 1.528, etc.}}]
**3** **clustered ear-ring**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 320.10.}}
**II** ={ἀμβροσία} and ἀρτεμισία, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 3.114.}}
**II.2** **oak of Jerusalem, Chenopodium Botrys**, {{Refs|[prev. work]115.}}
**III** **the Pleiades**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.486.}}

1010:
** βουλ-ευτής**, οῦ, ὁ,
**councillor, senator**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.114, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.5, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 25a, etc.}}; at Athens, **one of the**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|500, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.45, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.43, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 808}}; at Rome, **senator**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.12, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 13, etc.}}
**2** **plotter, contriver**, θανάτου, πληγῆς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 4.3.4.}}

1011:
** βουλ-εύω**,
{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.379, etc.}}, _aorist_ ἐβούλευσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.23, etc.}}, _Epic dialect_ βούλ- {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.464}}: _perfect_ βεβούλευκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 701}}:—_middle_ and _passive_, see below: (βουλή):—**take counsel, deliberate**, in past tenses, **determine** or **resolve after deliberation**:
**1** _absolutely_, ὣς βουλεύσαντε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.531}}; βουλευέμεν ἠδὲ μάχεσθαι **in council** or in battle, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.491; β. ὅπως ὄχ᾽ ἄριστα γένοιτο 9.420, compare 11.229; δυσμενέεσσι φόνου πέρι β. 16.234}}; ἔς γε μίαν βουλεύσομεν (i.e. βουλήν) we **shall agree** to one plan, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.379; θυμῷ β. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 12.58; β. περί τινος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.120, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.28, 5.116}}: in Prose, chiefly _middle_ in this sense, see below {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus}}
**2** with _accusative_ _of things_, **deliberate on, plan, devise**, β. βουλάς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.652, +others}}; οὐ.. τοῦτον μὲν ἐβούλευσας νόον αὐτ; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.23; ὁδόν 1.444; φύξιν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.311, 398; κέρδεα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 23.217; ψεύδεα 14.296: with _dative_ _person_, τῷ γάρ ῥα θεοὶ βούλευσαν ὄλεθρον 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.464, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.110; θάνατόν τινι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 872a; β. πῆμά τινι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.179, etc.; κέλευθον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 758; ποινάς [prev. author] “Ag.” 1223; νεώτερα β. περί τινος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.210}}:—_passive_ (with _future_ _middle_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 198)}}, _aorist_ ἐβουλεύθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.157, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.120, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 442b:}}_perfect_ βεβούλευμαι (usually in _middle_ sense, see infr. B):—**to be determined** or **planned**, ψῆφος κατ᾽ αὐτῶν βουλεύσεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus [same place]; βεβούλευται τάδε [prev. author] “Pr.” 998, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.10}}.; τὰ βεβουλευμένα, ={βουλεύματα}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 4.128; τὰ βουλευόμενα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 6.2.2; πῶς σφῷν βεβούλευται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 274a.}}
**3** with _infinitive_, **take counsel, resolve** to do, τὸν μὲν ἐγὼ βούλευσα.. οὐτάμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.299, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.73, 6.52, 61, etc.}}:— _passive_, τοῖσι ἐβεβούλευτο τὸ παιδίον προσουδίσαι {{Refs|[prev. author] 5.92.γ.}}
**II** **give counsel**, τὰ λῷστα β. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 206; β. δυνατός 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 694b}}: with _dative_ _person_, **advise**, ἵνα σφίσι βουλεύησθα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.99, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 697.}}
**III** **sit in council**, of the Spartan γέροντες, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.57}}; **to be a member of a** βουλή, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1282a30}}; especially **of the Council of** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|500 at Athens, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.45, 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.75, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.1.18, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 62.3; ἡ βουλὴ ἡ βουλεύουσα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 13.19}}; βουλὴν β. **to be a member of the** β., {{Refs|[prev. work]20}}; βουλεύειν λαχών {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 473e.}}
**B** _middle_, _future_ **-εύσομαι**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 846, “Ch.” 718, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.43, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 174d}}: _aorist_ ἐβουλευσάμην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 537, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ βουλ- {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.114; ἐβουλεύθην 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 15.7}}: _perfect_ βεβούλευμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.134, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 385, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.69, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Supplices” 248, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 176c (also in _passive_ sense, see above):}} —more frequently in _Attic dialect_ Prose than _active_,
**B.1** _absolutely_, **take counsel with oneself, deliberate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.10.δ, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1112b11, 20; παραχρῆμα οὐδὲ -σάμενος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 37.13; ἅμα τινί 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.101; περὶ τοῦ μέλλοντος τῶν οἰκείων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.44, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 231a; περί τι [prev. author] “R.” 604c}}; ὑπέρ τινος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 428d; πρὸς τὴν γεγενημένην ξυμφοράν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.47: with _accusative_ _cognate_, β. βούλευμα 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 3.29; βουλήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 298b, etc.; ἴσον τι ἢ δίκαιον 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.44}}:—also like _active_, **take counsel**, πρός τινας {{LXX|LXX.4Ki.6.8.}}
**B.2** **act as member of council**, and so **originate measures**, β. καὶ κρίνειν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1281b31; τὸ βουλευόμενον [prev. work] 1291a28.}}
**B.3** with _accusative_ _of things_, **determine with oneself, resolve on**, κακὴν ἀπάτην βουλεύσατο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.114}} (_middle_ here only in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus); ἀλλοῖόν τι περί τινος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.40, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 32c.}}
**B.4** with _infinitive_, **resolve** to do, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.134, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 176c.}}
**B.5** rarely followed by Relat., β. ὅ τι ποιήσεις {{Refs|[prev. passage]}}; β. ὅπως.. with _subjunctive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.13}}; β. πῶς τις, with _future_, {{NT+2nd c.BC+|[prev. author] “An.” 3.4.40; πῶς καὶ τί πρακτέον εἴη 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.33.3; ἵνα NT.John.12.10.}}

1012:
** βουλή**, ἡ,
_Doric dialect_ **βωλά** Decrees Byzantine cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.90}}, _Aeolic dialect_ **βόλλα** {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“Schwyzer” 623.1 (2nd c.BC), 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.288b}}: _accusative_ _plural_ βουλᾰς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 534}}: (βούλομαι):— **will, determination**, especially of the gods, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.5, etc.}}
**2** **counsel, design**, βουλὰς βουλεύουσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 24.652, etc.}}: generally, **counsel, advice**, opposed to μάχεσθαι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.258, compare 2.202, etc.; κακὴ β. 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 266; πρᾶτος.. καὶ βουλᾷ καὶ χερσὶν ἐς Ἄρεα “IG” 9(1).658 (from Ithaca)}}; νυκτὶ βουλὴν διδόναι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.12 (but ἐν νυκτὶ β. διδοὺς ἐμαυτῷ 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 35); ἐν β. ἔχειν τὰ γενόμενα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.78}}; β. ποιεῖσθαι, ={βουλεύεσθαι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 6.101, etc.; β. διδόναι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.26; β. προτιθέναι περί τινος 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.192; β. ἄγειν 2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 7.39}}; ἐν βουλῇ γενέσθαι πότερον.. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 2.44}}; τούτοις οὐκ ἔστι κοινὴ β. they have no common **ground of argument**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Crito” 49d; βουλῆς ὀρθότης ἡ εὐβουλία 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1142b16}}: in _plural_, **counsels**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 221, “Th.” 842 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐν βουλαῖς ἄριστος, ἐν βουλαῖσι κράτιστος, {{Refs|“epigram Gr.” 854, “IG” 3.716.}}
**3** **deliberation**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1112a19, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 9.46.}}
**4** **decree**, β. εἰσηγεῖσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.61; β. ἄκυρον θεῖναι [prev. author] 2.28.}}
**II** **Council of elders, Senate**, βουλὴν ἷζε γερόντων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.53, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.127, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 884; especially at Athens, “Council”}} or **Senate of** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|500 created by Cleisthenes, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.5, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 590, 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 6.40, etc.}}; commonly called ἡ β. (or ἡ β. οἱ πεντακόσιοι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.20, to distinguish it from ἡ β. ἡ ἐν Ἀρείῳ πάγῳ [prev. passage]}}; also β. ἀπὸ κυάμου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 8.66)}}; in other states, as at Argos, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.149; at Thebes, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.2.29}}; of the Roman {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Senate”, 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 6.69, etc.}}; of local **senates,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|POxy. 58.14 (3rd c.AD), etc.}}; βουλῆς εἶναι to be **of the Council, a member of it**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.70 (whence Scholia and 10th c.AD: Suidas Legal icographus}} made a _substantive_ βουλῆς, ὁ); ἀνὴρ βουλῆς τῆς Ῥωμαίων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 5.20.8; ἄνδρα ἐκ τῆς βουλῆς [prev. author] 7.11.1.}}

1013:
** βούλ-ημα**, ατος, τό,
**purpose**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Gorgias Rhetor et Sophista “Hel.” 6 (_plural_), 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 993, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.15, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.49 (_plural_)}}; **intent**, τοῦ νομοθέτου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 769d, 802c (_plural_), +others}}; τὸ β. τῆς κρίσεως **intention** to judge, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phlb.” 41e: _plural_, βουλήμασι Μοίρης “IG” 12(7).303.}}
**2** **meaning**, οὐδεὶς σαφῶς παρέδωκε τὸ β. {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Tactica - Griechische Kriegsschriftsteller” 18.1; τὸ β. τοῦ ποιητοῦ 2nd c.BC: Hipparchus Astronomicus 1.4.9, +others}}
**3** **intention of a testator**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 361ii 23 (2nd c.AD)}}: hence, **will, testament,** {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|POxy. 907.1 (3rd c.AD), “PLips.” 29.7 (3rd c.AD).}}
**II** **express will, consent**, τῆς συγκλήτου {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.15.4.}}

1014:
** βούλομαι**
(_Epic dialect_ also βόλομαι, which see), _Doric dialect_ **βώλ-** (which see), _Aeolic dialect_ **βόλλ-** (see. βόλομαι), Thess. **βέλλ-** {{Refs|“IG” 9(2).517.20}}, _Boeotian dialect_ **βείλ-** {{Refs|[prev. work]7.3080}}**βήλ-** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“SIG” 1185.18 (Tanagra, 3rd c.BC)}}, _Locrian dialect_and Delph. **δείλ-** {{Refs|“IG” 9(1).334.3, “GDI” 2034.10}}, Coan, etc. **δήλ-** (which see), _Ionic dialect_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ βούλεαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.364, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.11}}: _imperfect_ ἐβουλόμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 11.79, etc.; ἠβουλόμην 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 752, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 1.15, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἐβουλέατο codices in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.4, 3.143}}: _future_ βουλήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 867, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1077, etc.}}; later _future_ βουληθήσομαι variant in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Aristides Rhetor “Orationes” 48(24).8, 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus}} 13.636: _aorist_ ἐβουλήθην, also ἠβ- (see. below), βουληθείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 732, “IG” 22.1236, etc}}., but _Epic dialect_ _aorist_ _subjunctive_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ βούλεται (from *βόλσ-ε-ται) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.67}}: _perfect_ βεβούλημαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.2}}; also βέβουλα (προ-) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.113 (ἐβέβουλε}} uncertain in epigram in {{Refs|“Berl.Sitzb.” 1894.907):}} — forms with augment ἠ- are found in _Attic dialect_ Inscrr. from {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC onwards, as “IG” 22.657, +others, and occur frequently in Mss. as ἠβούλοντο variant in 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.2, 6.79, ἠβούλου 4th c.BC: Hyperides Orator “pro Lycophrone” 11}}; said to be Ionic in {{Refs|“Anecdota Graeca - MSS. Bibl. Oxon.” 2.374.}} — An _active_ βούλητε (={βούλησθε}) Mitteis {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“Chr.” 361.10 (4th c.AD)}}:—**will, wish, be willing**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.: usually implying choice or preference (cf. IV) opposed to ἐθέλω}} 'consent', εἰ βούλει, ἐγὼ ἐθέλω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 522e, compare “R.” 347b, 437b; ἐὰν βούλῃ σὺ.. ἐὰν θεὸς ἐθέλῃ [prev. author] Alcaeus1.135d; ἂν οἵ τε θεοὶ 'θέλωσι καὶ ὑμεῖς βούλησθε 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.20; οὔτ᾽ ἀκούειν ἠθέλετ᾽ οὔτε πιστεύειν ἐβούλεσθε [prev. author] 19.23}}; but ἐθέλω is also used = 'wish', λέξαι θέλω σοι, πρὶν θανεῖν, ἃ βούλομαι, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 281 (so ἐθέλω εἰπεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 309b, but φράσαι τι βούλομαι 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 1090): 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus uses βούλομαι for ἐθέλω}} in the case of the gods, for with them **wish is will**: ἐθέλω is more general, and is sometimes used where βούλομαι might have stood, e.g. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.182.}}—Construct.: mostly with _infinitive_, Τρώεσσιν ἐβούλετο κῦδος ὀρέξαι {{Refs|11.79, etc.}}; sometimes with _infinitive_ _future_, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 184}}; with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.353, and frequently in Prose: when βούλομαι is followed by _accusative_ only, an}}_infinitive_ may generally be supplied, as καί κε τὸ βουλοίμην (i.e. γενέσθαι) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.316}}; ἔτυχεν ὧν ἐβούλετο (i.e. τυχεῖν) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 18.6}}; πλακοῦντα β. (i.e. σε λέγειν) {{Refs|[prev. author] 52.11}}; καὶ εἰ μάλα βούλεται ἄλλῃ [Ποσειδάων] (i.e. τοῦτο γενέσθαι) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.51}}; so εἰς τὸ βαλανεῖον βούλομαι (i.e. ἰέναι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1279}}; βουλοίμην ἄν (i.e. τόδε γενέσθαι) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 3a.}}
**2** in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus of gods, with _accusative_ _of things_ et _dative_ _person_, Τρώεσσιν.. ἐβούλετο νίκην}} he **willed** victory to the Trojans, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.21, compare 23.682: later with _accusative_, τὸ βουλόμενον τὴν πολιτείαν πλῆθος}} **that supports** the constitution, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1309b17.}}
**II** _Attic dialect_ usages:
**II.1** βούλει or βούλεσθε followed by Verb in _subjunctive_, βούλει λάβωμα; **would you have** me take hold? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 761}}; βούλει φράσ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 36, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 79a, “R.” 596a}}; ποῦ δὴ βούλει ἀναγνῶμε; {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 228e.}}
**II.2** εἰ βούλει if **you please**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1168, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.41}}; also εἰ δὲ βούλει, ἐὰν δὲ βούλῃ, to express a concession, or if **you like**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 201a, etc.; εἰ μὲν β., φρονήσει, εἰ δὲ β., ἰσχύι [prev. author] “R.” 432a.}}
**II.3** ὁ βουλόμενος **any one who likes**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.54, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.26, etc.; ἔδωκεν ἅπαντι τῷ βουλομένῳ 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.45}}; ὁ β. the 'common in former', {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 918 (whence, in jest, βούλομαι [prev. work] 908)}}; ὅστις βούλει who or which ever **you like**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 517b, “Cra.” 432a.}}
**II.4** βουλομένῳ μοί ἐστι, with _infinitive_, it is **according to** my **wish** that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.3; εἰ σοὶ β. ἐστὶν ἀποκρίνεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 448d; also τὰ θεῶν οὕτω βουλόμεν᾽ ἔσται 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 33}}; τὸ κεἰνου βουλόμενον his **wish**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1270}}; but with _passive_ sense, τὸ β. the **object of desire**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 37, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Artoxerxes” 28.}}
**II.5** τί βουλόμενο; **with** what **purpose?** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 63a, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.172; τί βουληθεὶς πάρε; 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1100.}}
**III** **mean**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 362e, 590e, etc.}}; τί ἡμῖν βούλεται οὗτος ὁ μῦθος**;** (followed by β. λέγειν ὡς..) {{Refs 1st c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tht.” 156c; τί β. σημαίνειν τὸ τέρας 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 4.59}}; βούλεται εἶναι **professes** or **pretends** to be, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 595c; β. τὸ ὄνομα ἐπικεῖσθαι [prev. author] “Cra.” 412c; frequently in 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus, τὸ ἀκούσιον βούλεται λέγεσθαι οὐκ εἰ.. “EN” 1110b30; β. ἄσωτος εἶναι ὁ ἕν τι κακὸν ἔχων [prev. work] 1119b34; β. ὁ πρᾶος ἀτάραχος εἶναι [prev. work] 1125b33}}; **tend** to be, ἡ τοῦ ὕδατος φύσις β. εἶναι ἄχυμος {{Refs|[prev. author] “Sens.” 441a3}}; β. ἤδη τότε εἶναι πόλις ὅταν.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pol.” 1261b12, compare 1293b40}}; ἡ φύσις β. μὲν τοῦτο ποιεῖν πολλάκις, οὐ μέντοι δύναται{{Refs|[prev. work] 1255b3, compare “GA” 778a4, +others}}
**III.2** **to be wont**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.3.18.}}
**IV** followed by ἤ.., **prefer**, for βούλομαι μᾶλλον (which is more usually in Prose), βούλομ᾽ ἐγὼ λαὸν σόον ἔμμεναι ἢ ἀπολέσθαι I **had rather**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.117, compare 23.594, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.232, 11.489, 12.350; β. τὸ μέν τι εὐτυχέειν.. ἢ εὐτυχέειν τὰ πάντα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.40}}; β. παρθενεύεσθαι πλέω χρόνον ἢ πατρὸς ἐστερῆσθαι (for πολὺν χρόνον, μᾶλλον ἤ{{Refs 5th c.BC+|..) [prev. work] 124, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 351}}; less frequently without ἤ.., πολὺ βούλομαι αὐτὴν οἴκοι ἔχειν I much **prefer**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.112, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.88}}. (**g[uglide]el-g[uglide]ol-**, compare the dialectic forms.)

1015:
** βουνός**, ὁ,
**hill, mound**, Cyrenaic word, _accusative_ to {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.199; frequently in Syracusan poets, _accusative_ to 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 333, compare 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 49, 142, LXX.Exo.17.9, +others, 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 3.83.1, “Schwyzer” 289.168 (Rhodian, 2nd c.BC), 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 3.2.9, “BGU” 1129.14 (1st c.BC), etc.}}
**2** **heap** of stones, etc., {{LXX|LXX.Gen.31.46; σίτου “PFlor.” 58.12.}}
**II** **clot** of blood, [{{Refs 5th c.AD+|Cyrilli Glossarium see entry {θρόμβος},compare 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus see at {θρόμβοι}.}}]
**III** **altar**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**IV** ={στιβάς} {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|(5th c.AD: Cypria), [prev. author] (Barbarous word _accusative_ to 2nd c.AD: Aelius Dionysius Grammaticus “fragment” 93.)}}

1016:
** βοῦς**,
_Doric dialect_ **βῶς** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 9.7, “GDI” 5005.5 (from Gortyn), ὁ and ἡ: _genitive_ βοός (written βοιός “GDI” iv p.883 No.62 (from Erythrae))}}, _poetry_ also βοῦ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 421, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 280: _accusative_ βοῦν “IG” 12.45 A 11, etc., βῶν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.238}} and _Doric dialect_, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 4.914.18, +others (Epid.), “SIG” 56.16 (from Argos), 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 27.64}}, _Ionic dialect_ and _poetry_ also βόα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Pherecydes Lerius Historicus 162 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus, “Anthologia Graeca” 9.255 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus): dual βόε 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 436: _plural_, _nominative_ βόες}}, rarely _contraction_ βοῦς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 760, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Aemilius Paulus” 33, etc.: _genitive_ βοῶν}}, _contraction_ βῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 983; Bocot. βουῶν “IG” 7.3171.45: _dative_ βουσί}}, _Epic dialect_ βόεσσι, βοσί {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.622 (1st c.AD: Antiphilus Epigrammaticus)}}; _Boeotian dialect_ βούεσσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|“IG” 7.3171.38: _accusative_ βόας 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.556, +others, βοῦς 1.154, +others, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 175 (Lyric poetry)}}, and _Attic dialect_, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 172.5, etc. (but later βόας NT.John.2.14, “POxy.” 729.16 (2nd c.AD), etc.)}}:—**bullock, bull, ox**, or **cow**, in _plural_ **cattle**, commonly _feminine_: to mark the male {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} adds a word, β. ἄρσην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.495; or ταῦρος β. 17.389}}; as a measure of value, βοὸς ἄξιον [{{Refs|23.885, compare 7.474 and see at {ἀλφεσίβοιος},{ἑκατόμβοιος}.}}]
**b** βοῦς ἄγριος **buffalo**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 499a4.}}
**c** βοῦς ἐν Συρίᾳ **zebu**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 606a15}}; β. ἐν Παιονίᾳ, perhaps **urus**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mir.” 842b33.}}
**d** βοὸς ὄμμα, ={βούφθαλμος}, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 4.1.52 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus).}}
**2** _metaphorically_ of any **dam** or **mother**, μία β. Κρηθεῖ τε μάτηρ καὶ Σαλμωνεῖ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.142; ἄπεχε τῆς β. τὸν ταῦρον 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1125.}}
**II** ={βοείη} or βοέη (always _feminine_), **ox-hide shield**, νωμῆσαι βῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.238; τυκτῇσι βόεσσιν 12.105}}; βόας αὔας{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 137; γέρρα λευκῶν β. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.4.12.}}
**III** a **fish**, perhaps **Notidanus griseus**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 540b17, “fragment” 280.}}
**III.2** a fish of the Nile, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 17.2.4.}}
**IV** ἔβδομος β. **crescent loaf**, {{Refs|Clidem. 16.}}
**V** **seam**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 7.65.}}
**VI** **the constellation Taurus**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC(?): Maximus Astrologus 162.}}
**VII** ={μάστιξ}, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**VIII** proverbial, β. ἐπὶ γλώσσης ἐπιβαίνων {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 815; βοῦς ἐπὶ γλώσσῃ βέβηκε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 36}}; of people who keep silence from some weighty reason, τὸν βοῦν ἐπιτίθημι τῇ γλώττῃ {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 7.218a; βοῦς ἐμβαίνει μέγας Strattis 67}} (wrongly explained by {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Zenobius Paroemiographus 2.70, etc., of bribery with coins bearing type of ox); β. ἐν πόλει 'bull in a china-shop', Diogcnian. 3.67}}; β. ἐν αὐλίῳ, of a useless person, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 32}}; β. λύρας 'pearls before swine', {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Macho Comicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 8.349c. (βοῦς (from βωύς}}, Sanskrit _gaús_) _accusative_ βῶν (Sanskrit _gā´m_) are old forms: stem βωϝ-βοϝ-, cf. Latin (Umbr.) **bos**, etc.)

1017:
** βρᾰβ-εῖον**,
_Epic dialect_ **βρᾰβ-ήϊον**, τό, **prize in the games**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 653, NT.1Cor.9.24, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.742c, 3rd c.AD: Oppianus Apamensis Epicus “Cynegetica” 4.197.}}
**II** **wand, baton** given as a prize, τιμηθεὶς χρυσείῳβ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 3674 (Cyzicus, 2nd c.AD),compare “IG” 14.748 (from Naples).}}

1018:
** βρᾰβ-εύω**,
**act as judge** or **umpire**, ἐν τῇ κληρώσει τὴν τύχην βραβεύσειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.23}}; ὀρθῶς β. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Axiopistus Poeta Ethicus 2.7}}; **preside** at an election, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Mi.” 44.}}
**II** with _accusative_, **arbitrate, decide on**, τὰ τῶν ἄλλων δίκαια {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 3.27, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ἀθηναίων Πολιτεία” 9.2; κρίσεις 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cicero” 35}}; δικαστήριον{{LXX+1st c.AD+|[prev. work] 9; ἅμιλλαν [prev. author] 2.960a, etc.: with _accusative_ et _dative_, ἀγῶνα ἰσχυρὸν ἐβράβευσεν αὐτῷ LXX.Wis.10.12; προσήκει βασιλεῖ τὰ δίκαια β. τοῖς ὑπηκόοις 1st c.AD: Musonius Philosophus 8p.33H.}}:—_passive_, βραβευθήσεται τὰ τοῦ πολέμου τῷ νενικηκότι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 6.9.1; τὰ παρά τν ι βραβευόμενα 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.70; συνέβη.. τὴν κρίσιν βραβευθῆναι “SIG” 685.37 (Cretan dialect).}}
**II.2** **direct, control,** {{Refs 1st c.BC+|Michel 163.10 (from Delos), “Anthologia Graeca” 12.56 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus); νοῦς β. πάντα 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.94}}:—_passive_, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 6.4.3, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pelopidas” 13; πάντων ὑπὸ τοῦ δαιμονίου -ομένων 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Herc.” 1251.7.}}

1019:
** βρᾰδύνω**,
_future_ **-ῠνῶ**{{LXX|LXX.Deu.7.10}}: _aorist_ ἐβράδῡνα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Cont.” 1, 2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 1.69}}: _pluperfect_ ἐβεβραδύκειν {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Symp.” 20}}: (βραδύς):
**I** _transitive_, **make slow, delay**, {{LXX|LXX.Isa.46.13}}:—_passive_, **to be delayed**, τἀπὸ σοῦ βραδύνεται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1628; ἡ δ᾽ ὁδὸς βραδύνεται [prev. author] “El.” 1501.}}
**II** _intransitive_, **loiter, delay**, εἰ βραδύνοιμεν βοῇ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 730}} (so in _middle_, χεῖρα δ᾽ οὐ βραδύνεται.. ἁρπάσαι δόρυ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Th.” 623); μὴ βράδυνε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1400; σπεύδων.. βραδύνω 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 528d}}: with _infinitive_, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 1.48.4}}; βραδύνει σοι τοῦτ; **are** you **slow, slack** in this ? {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 1.6.}}

1021:
** βρᾰδύς**, εῖα, ύ:
_comparative_ βραδύτερος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.8; _metathesis_ βαρδύτερος 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 29.30; βραδίων 2nd c.AD: Artemidorus Daldianus Onirocriticus 1.70}}: _superlative_ βραδύτατος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 357, also βράδιστος (_metathesis_ βάρδιστος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.310, 530, Doroth(?).cited in 7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus “Astr.” 3.30) 2nd c.AD: Aretaeus Medicus “περὶ αἰτιῶν καὶ σημείων χρονίων παθῶν” 1.6, βραδίστατος 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Fragmenta” 325}}:—**slow**, κιχάνει τοι β. ὠκύν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.329, etc.: with}}_infinitive_, ἀλλά τοι ἵπποι βάρδιστοι θείειν **slowest** at running, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.310; β. λέγειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 237, etc.}}; τὸ β. **delay**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 766e}}. _adverb_ βραδέως, χωρεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.70; θεῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 336a, etc.}}: _comparative_ **-ύτερον**{{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “προγνωστικόν” 22, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 190a; βραδυτέρως 4th c.BC: Aeneas Tacticus 16.12; βράδῐον 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 528, 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus 1.117 (condemned by 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Sol.” 7)}}: _superlative_ **-ύτατα**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 39b.}}
**2** of the mind, **dull, sluggish**, ἐπιλήσμων καί β. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 129}}; opposed to ἀγχίνους, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 239a}}; βράδιστοι τὴν γνώμην Aret.[same place]: with _infinitive_, προνοῆσαι βραδεῖς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.38}}; τὸ β. καὶ μέλλον **slowness** and deliberation, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.84.}} _adverb_ βραδέως, βουλεύεσθαι{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 78; β. ὀλίγην ὀργὴν ποιεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 233c.}}
**3** in Egypt, of illiterates, βραδέως, βραδύτερον γράφειν, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PTeb.” 316.101 (1st c.AD), “PRyl.” 173.13 (1st c.AD)}}; also βραδέα γράφουσα {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“BGU” 446.19 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** of Time, **tardy, late**, σὺν χρόνῳ β. μολών {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 395, compare 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.43; βραδεῖαν.. ὁδὸν πέμπων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 738}}. _adverb_, ἕως βραδέως ἦν τῆς ἡμέρας {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 2.139}}: _neuter_ as _adverb_, ὀψὲ καὶ βραδὺ τῆς ἡλικίας {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 2.29; βράδιον ἀπογαλακτίζειν 2nd c.AD: Soranus Medicus [same place]}}

1024:
** βρᾰχύς**, εῖα
(_Ionic dialect_ έα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.49)}}, ύ, _dative_ _plural_ βραχέοις {{Refs|“JHS” 33.317 (Thess.): _comparative_ βραχύτερος, βραχίων (compare βράσσων): _superlative_ βραχύτατος, βράχιστος}}:—**short**,
**1** of Space and Time, β. οἶμος, ὁδός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.248, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 718e, etc.}}; [αἰών] probably in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 3.74; βίος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.46; καιρός 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 9; χρόνος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 939, “Pers.” 713, etc.}}; μῦθος, λόγος, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Pr.” 505, variant in “Pers.” 713}}; ἐν βραχεῖ (_Ionic dialect_ βραχέϊ) in **a short time**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.24, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 217a codices; διὰ βραχέος 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.83; μακρὰν συνήθειαν βραχεῖ λῦσαι χρόνῳ 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 726}}; βραχὺ τῃδὶ μεταστῶμεν **for a moment**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Georg.” 32}}; of distance, β. ἀπόδοσις **short return** in ballplay, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 234.6; ἐπὶ βραχὺ ἐξικνεῖσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.3.17}}; πρὸ βραχέος lamb.{{Refs|“VP” 25.112}}: _comparative_, ἡ φάλαγξ -υτέρα ἐγένετο ἀναδιπλουμένη {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.5.5; τάξιν -υτέραν ἢ πρόσθεν, βαθυτέραν δὲ ποιήσαντες 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.33.10; βραχύτερα τοξεύειν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.3.7.}} _adverb_ βραχέως, [πολέμους] ἐπ᾽ ἀλλήλους ἐπιφέρειν **scantily, seldom**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.141.}}
**2** of Size, **short, small**, μορφάν β. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 4(3).53}}; βραχὺς ἐξικέσθαι θεῶν ἕδραν too **puny** to reach..,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 7(6).44; β. τεῦχος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1113, compare 757}}; β. τεῖχος a **low** wall, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.29}}; βραχύ μοι στόμα πάντ᾽ ἀναγήσασθαι my mouth is **too small** to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 10.19}}; κατὰ β. προϊών **little by little**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.64, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 241c}}; παρὰ βραχύ **scarcely, hardly**, φυγεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.5; βραχύ τι λωφᾶν ἀπὸ νόσου καὶ πολέμου 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.12}}; ἁλὸς βραχύ **a small quantity** of salt, Bilabel Ὀψαρτ.{{Refs|p.11.}}
**3** of Number, **few**, ἐν βραχεῖ in **few words**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 1.82, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 673; ἐν βραχίστοις 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 6(5).59; ἐν βραχυτέροις 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 449c}}; so διὰ βραχέων in **few words**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Prt.” 336a; ὡς ἂν δύνωμαι διὰ βραχυτάτων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 27.3, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 16.9, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 449c; ὡς ἐν βραχυτάτοις 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 1.18.}} _adverb_ βραχέως, ἀπολογεῖσθαι **briefly, in few words**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.7.5.}}
**4** of Value or Importance, of persons, **humble, insignificant**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 880; τὸν μὲν ἀφ᾽ ὑψηλῶν βραχὺν ᾤκισε 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 613; β. τὴν διάνοιαν 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 12.4.1}}; of things, **petty, trifling**, ἀρχὴ β. ἐλπίδος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 121; χάρις [prev. author] “Tr.” 1217; πρόφασις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1180}}; β. τις ἀσάφεια a **slight** obscurity, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(1).304}}; λυπεῖν τινὰ βραχύ, opposed to μέγ᾽ εὑρεῖν κέρδος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1304; οὐ περὶ βραχέων βουλεύεσθαι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.78, compare 140; β. καὶ οὐδενὸς ἄξιον [prev. author] 8.76; β. κέρδους ἕνεκα 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 7.17; οὐσία 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 10.25: _neuter_ as}} _adverb_, βραχὺ φροντίζειν τινός think **lightly** of, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 17.4.}}
**5** **short**, of vowels or syllables, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Categoriae” 4b34, “Rh.” 1409a18, “Po.” 1458a15, 7th c.AD: Hephaestio Grammaticus 1.1, 2nd c.BC: Dionysius Thrax Grammaticus 631, etc.}}; ἡ β. προσῳδία the sign ?~X, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Sextus Empiricus Philosophus “adversus Mathematicos” 1.113}}. (Cf. Avest. **m[schwa]r[schwa]zu-** 'short', Gothic _gamaurgjan_ 'shorten', Latin _brevis._)

1025:
** βρέφος**, εος, τό,
**babe in the womb, foetus**, β. ἡμίονον κυέουσαν, of a mare, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.266, compare 3rd c.BC: Chrysippus Stoicus 2.222.}}
**II** **new-born babe**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 37.15, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.33, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 1096 (Lyric poetry); νέον β. 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 289 [not in 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus]: in later Prose, LXX.Sir.19.11, “BGU” 1104.24 (1st c.BC)}}, etc.; of beasts, **foal, whelp, cub**, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.153, 3rd c.BC: Phylarchus Historicus 36, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 3.8, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 5.464, etc.}}; **nestling**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Horapollo 2.99}}; ἐκ βρέφεος from **babyhood,** {{NT|Anthologia Graeca 9.567 (Antip.); ἀπὸ β. NT.2Tim.3.15}}. (Cf. Slavonic _žrèbę_ 'foal'.)

1026:
** βρέχω**,
_future_ **-ξω**{{LXX+2nd c.AD+|LXX.Amos.4.7, +others, (ἀπο-) 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.591, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἔβρεξα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.78, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 254c, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.3.12, etc.}}:—_passive_, _future_ βρᾰχήσομαι {{LXX|LXX.Isa.34.3}}: _aorist_ ἐβρέχθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 326, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.4.17, etc.:}}_aorist 2_ ἐβράχην [ᾰ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.80, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 906b26, 1st-2nd c.AD: Sotion Paradoxographus p.190 W., 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.270, “Anacreontea” 31.26; but ἐβρέχην “PGiss.” 160v12 (2nd c.AD), Wilcken “Chr.” 341.6 (2nd c.AD)}}: _perfect_ βέβρεγμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.55, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Acut.(Sp.”)}} 47:—**wet**, of persons walking through water, τὸ γόνυ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.189; τοὺς πόδας 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 229a}}; **steep** in water, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 3; ἐν οἴνῳ [prev. author] “Fract.” 29}}; β. χρυσέαις νιφάδεσσι πόλιν **shower** wealth upon it, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 7.34; δακρύοισιν ἔβρεξαν ὅλον τάφον “IG” 14.1422; β. ἐν δάκρυσι τὴν στρωμνήν LXX.Psa.6.7, compare 77(78).27}}:—_passive_, **get wet**, βρεχόμενοι πρὸς τὸν ὀμφαλόν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.5.2}}; βρέχεσθαι ἐν ὕδατι **to be bathed** in sweat or **drench themselves**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.104 (so ἱδρῶτι β. τὴν ψυχήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 254c)}}; βεβρεγμένος **filled with water**, opposed to διερός, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione et Corruptione” 330a17}}; of sponges, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Mete.” 386b5; ἄλφιτα β. ἐν ὕδατι 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 2.110}}; **to be rained upon**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 16.12.3; ὄμβροις 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 15.1.13}}; especially in Egypt of the inundation of the Nile, τὰ βρεχέντα πεδία {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PFlor.” 331.6 (2nd c.AD)}}; ἡ βεβρεγμένη (i.e. γῆ) {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 71.2 (2nd c.BC), “OGI” 669.57 (1st c.AD)}}; γῆ οὐ βρεχομένη {{LXX|LXX.Eze.22.24}}:—but also _intransitive_ in _active_, **to be inundated,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PPetr. 3p.119 (3rd c.BC), “PTeb.” 106.19 (2nd c.BC): _metaphorically_, ἀκτῖσι βεβρεγμένος}} **steeped, bathed in** light, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.55; σιγᾷ βρέχεσθαι [prev. author] “fragment” 240}}; of hard drinkers, μέθῃ βρεχθείς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 326}}; βεβρεγμένος **tipsy**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 126.}}
**II** **rain, send rain, **{{NT|NT.Matt.5.45}}; Ζεὺς ἔβρεχε {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 1482.6 (2nd c.AD)}}: with _accusative_, ἔβρεξε Κύριος χάλαζαν {{LXX|LXX.Exo.9.23}}; θεῖον{{LXX+NT|LXX.Gen.19.24, cf. NT.Luke.17.29}}; ἄρτους {{LXX|Al. LXX.Exo.16.4.}}
**II.2** _impersonal_, βρέχει **it rains**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Teleclides Comicus 54, NT.Jam.5.17; ὅταν βρέχῃ 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 1.6.26; also ἵνα ὑετὸς βρέχῃ NT.Rev.11.6.}}

1027:
** βροντ-ή**, ἡ,
**thunder**, Διὸς μεγάλοιο κεραυνὸν δεινήν τε β. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.199; ὑπὸ βροντῆς πατρὸς Διός 13.796; Ζηνός τε βροντῇ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.121}}; ἀστραπὴ καὶ β. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.86; β. στεροπῇ τε 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 34 (_anapaest meter_); β. καὶ κεραυνίᾳ φλογί [prev. author] “Pr.” 1017; βροντῆς μύκημα [prev. work] 1062 (_anapaest meter_), compare 1083 (_anapaest meter_)}}; β. δ᾽ ἐρράγη δι᾽ ἀστραπῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 578, etc.: in _plural_, [prev. author] “OC” 1514, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 1.6.28, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Signis Tempestatum” 21, etc.}}; χθόνιαι β. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1745: _metaphorically_, τούτου τὰς β. οἶδ᾽ ὅτι δείσεις 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Epistulae” 98.4.}}
**II** **the state of one struck with thunder, astonishment**, ἐπεάν σφι θεὸς ἐμβάλῃ β. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.10.έ.}} (βρομτᾱ, compare βρέμω.)

1028:
** βροχή**, ἡ,
(βρέχω) **rain**, {{NT+1st c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 14.8, LXX.Psa.67(68).10, NT.Matt.7.25, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.48, “Geoponica” 2.39.7.}}
**II** **moistening**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.49, 3rd-4th c.AD: Philagrius Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 5.32.1, Mnesith.[prev. work] 8.35.11}}; **steeping**, in brewing, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PTeb.” 401.27 (1st c.AD).}}
**III** **inundation** of the Nile, in _plural_, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 280.5 (1st c.AD), 4th c.AD: Hephaestio Astrologus 1.23.}}
**III.2** **irrigation**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.6.3, “PPetr.” 3p.119 (3rd c.BC).}}

1030:
** βρυγ-μός**, ὁ,
**biting**, [prev. work = 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca”] 716 (_plural_, variant{βρυχμός}); **gobbling**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Eupolis Comicus 347}}; **chattering, shivering**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 3.84, “Steril.” 214, Euryphon cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 17(1).888}}; β. ὀδόντων **gnashing** of teeth, {{NT|NT.Matt.8.12, +others}}
**II** **roaring** of a lion, {{LXX|LXX.Pro.19.12.}}

1031:
** βρύκω**
or **βρύχω** [ῡ] (the former _Attic dialect_ _accusative_ to {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Moeris Grammaticus and 5th c.AD(?): Ammonius Epigrammaticus}}; the distinction βρύκω **bite**, βρύχω **gnash** does not hold good), mostly _present_: _future_ βρύξω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.2, 3rd c.BC: Lycophron Tragicus 678}}: _aorist_ ἔβρυξα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 5.86, 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 207, +others, “Anthologia Graeca” 7.624 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Diodorus Epigrammaticus), (ἐπ-) 5th-6th c.BC: Archippus Comicus 35}}: _aorist 2_ ἔβρῠχε {{Refs|“Anthologia Graeca” 9.252 (late, perhaps _imperfect_): for βέβρῡχα}}, see at {βρυχάομαι}: —_passive_, see below:—**eat greedily, gobble**, γνάθος ἱππείη βρύκει **champs** the bit, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus “Epigrammata” 14.13; ἑφθὰ καὶ ὀπτὰ [κρέα].. βρύκειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 358, compare 372; πρὸς ταῦτα βρύκετ᾽ 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 1315}}; **bite**, βρύκουσ᾽ ἀπέδεσθαι.. τοὺς δακτύλους **biting**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Av.” 26}}; of smoke, ὀδὰξ ἔβρυκε τὰς λήμας ἐμοῦ {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lys.” 301}}; later, simply, **devour, consume**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 489, +others}}; βρύξας, of the sea, is perhaps _falsa lectio_ for{βρόξας} in {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 7.624 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Diodorus Epigrammaticus)}}: _metaphorically_, **tear in pieces, devour**, of a gnawing disease, βρύκει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 987 (Lyric poetry); βρύκει γὰρ ἅπαν τὸ παρόν 5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 58; τὰ πατρῷα βρύκει 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 43.27}}:—_passive_, ἀπόλωλα, τέκνον, βρύκομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 745; βρυχθεὶς ἁλί “Anthologia Graeca” 9.267 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus).}}
**II** **gnash** or **grind** the teeth, τοὺς ὀδόντας βρύχει {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 1.7, etc., compare “Anthologia Graeca” 15.51 (1st c.BC: Archias); τὸ στόμα β. 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 95.45; β. τοὺς ὀδόντας ἐπί τινα NT.Act.7.54}}; also βρύχει alone, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 2.120}}; also _intransitive_, οἱ ὀδόντες βρύχουσι{{Refs 2nd c.BC+|[prev. work] 1.36; βρῦκον στόμα 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 226, compare “Th.” 207, +others}}:—_middle_, βρύχονται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἱερῆς νούσου” 1 (probably).}}

1032:
** βρύω** [ῠ],
mostly _present_: _imperfect_, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Pherecydes Syrius Philosophus cited in 3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 1.122, 2nd c.BC: Nicaenetus Epicus 7}}: _aorist_ _participle_ βρύσας {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus (see. below)}}:—**to be full to bursting**:
**1** with _dative_, **swell** or **teem with**, especially of plants, ἔρνος.. βρύει ἄνθεϊ λευκῷ **swells** with white bloom, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.56, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 107 (Lyric poetry); κισσῷ κάρα βρύουσαν 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 56.6}}; ἰούλῳ, θριξί, κόμαις, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Heroicus” 2.2, 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.31, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 12; γῆ φυτοῖς βρύουσα 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Mundo” 392b15; also βρύει ἱερὰ βουθύτοις ἑορταῖς 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 3.15: _metaphorically_, βίος.. βρύων μελίτταις καὶ προβάτοις κτλ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 45}}; of men, β. δόξᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 12.179; παμμάχῳ θράσει βρύων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 169 (Lyric poetry); ἀγαθοῖσι βρύοις [prev. author] “Supp.” 966 (_anapaest meter_); μαντικῇ β. τέχνῃ [prev. author] “fragment” 350.6; ἄλλων ἰατρὸς αὐτὸς ἕλκεσι βρύων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1086; β. ἄνθεσιν ἥβας 5th-6th c.BC: Timotheus Lyricus “Persae” 221; βρύουσαν ἀοιδὰν σοφίᾳ “Lyrica Alexandrina Adespota” 20.4; ἐμπόριον πλούτῳ βρύον 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.71d.}}
**2** with _genitive_, **to be full of**, χῶρος.. βρύων δάφνης ἐλαίας ἀμπέλου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 16; βρύοντα στέφανον μύρτων 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 329 (Lyric poetry)}}; στεφάνων δόμος ἔβρυεν probable reading in {{Refs 6th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicaenetus Epicus [same place]; τράπεζαν.. κόσμου βρύουσαν 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 86.3; καρπόν.. βρύειν σμαράγδου λίθου 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 5.5; τόπος β. ὕλης 1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 13.3.1; φθειρῶν ἔβρυον πᾶς 6th c.BC: Pherecydes Syrius Philosophus [same place]: _metaphorically_, νόσου β. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 70.}}
**3** _absolutely_, **abound, grow luxuriantly**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 422}}; of the earth, **teem with produce**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 5.12, compare 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 3.56}}; of water, **burst forth**, ὕδωρ βρύσαν ἐξ ὑπονόμων {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “Historia Arcana” 19.}}
**4** with _accusative_ _cognate_, **burst forth with, gush with**, γλυκύ, πικρὸν [ὕδωρ] {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Jam.3.11; τὴν γῆν τὰ οἰκεῖα βρύειν φησὶν ἀγαθά 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Fragmenta” 25; causal, Ὧραι β. λειμῶνας 4th c.AD: Himerius Sophista “Orationes” 1.19; ῥόδα “Anacreontea” 44.2.—poetical and later Prose.}}

1033:
** βρῶμα**, ατος, τό,
(βιβρώσκω) **that which is eaten, food, meat**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 6, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.26, +others: _metaphorically_, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 333: frequently in _plural_, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἀρχαίης ἰητρικῇς” 3, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 246, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 115b, etc.}}; opposed to ὄψα, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC(?): Sosipater Comicus 1.30.}}
**II** **cavity** in a tooth, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 4.25, Dse. 1.105, 2nd c.AD: Archigenes Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.859.}}
**II.2** **moth-eating**, in _plural_, {{LXX|LXX.EpJer.12.}}
**III** _plural_, **filth, ordure**, probably in {{NT|NT.Mark.7.19}}; compare βρῶμος (B).

1035:
** βρῶσις**, εως, ἡ,
_Epic dialect_ **βρώσ-ιος** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 797}}: (βιβρώσκω):— **meat**, opposed to πόσις, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.490, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης ὀξέων” 28, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.3.15, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 783c: in _plural_, opposed to πόσεις, 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 235; β. ἀναγκαία 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.70.}}
**2** **pasture**, προβάτων {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PLips.” 118.15 (2nd c.AD).}}
**II** **eating**, παίδων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 619c; ἐρινὸς ἀχρεῖος ὢν ἐς βρῶσιν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 181.}}
**II.2** **taste, flavour**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 377.}}
**II.3** **corrosion, rust,** {{NT|NT.Matt.6.19}}; **decay**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.422 (_plural_), 12.879.}}

1036:
** βῠθ-ίζω**,
**sink** a ship, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 552; μίαν πεντήρη ἐβύθισαν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.10.5}}; **let down**, ἀγκύρας {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Themistius Sophista “in Aristotelis Physica paraphrasis - pars” 133.20}}; **bury, plunge**, ἑρπετόν.. ἐν μυχῷ τοῦ πηλοῦ {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Alex.” 13}}:—_passive_, of a ship, etc., **sink**, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 11.18, 2nd c.AD: Babrius Fabularum Scriptor 117.1, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Caesar” 49, 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 63.3}}; of a person, **to be plunged into the sea**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.831d.}}
**II** **overwhelm, submerge**, of a flood, οἰκίας {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.306f}}:—_passive_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Daed.” 7.}}
**III** _metaphorically_, β. ἀνθρώπους εἰς ὄλεθρον {{NT|NT.1Tim.6.9}}:—_passive_, **to be ruined**, τοὺς διὰ φιλοκερδίαν βυθιζομένους {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “de Morte - Papiri Ercolanesi” 33; τὸ νῆφον ὑπὸ τοῦ πάθους βυθίζεται 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 1.13; νοῦς βυθισθεὶς θυμῷ ἢ ἐπιθυμίᾳ 6th c.AD: Simplicius Philosophus “in Aristotelis Physica commentaria - Comm. in Arist. Graeca” 273.11, compare 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 7.12.}}

1037:
** βῠθός**, ὁ,
**the depth**, especially of the sea, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 432 (Lyric poetry), NT.2Cor.11.25.}}
**b** generally, συνιζάνειν εἰς β. sink to the **bottom**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “de Odoribus” 29: _metaphorically_, ἐξ οὐρίων δραμοῦσαν ἐς βυθὸν πεσεῖν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1083; ἀνακουφίσαι κάρα βυθῶν [prev. author] “OT” 24}}; ἐκ βυθοῦ κηκῖον αἷμα from the **deep wound**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ph.” 783; καταφέρεσθαι εἰς β. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 619a7, etc.; τὴν ἀναφορὰν ποιησάμενος ἐκ τοῦ β. [prev. work] 622b7; ἐν τῷ β. τῆς θαλάττης [prev. work] 537a8: _metaphorically_, ἐν βυθῷ ἀτεχνίης}} in **the depth** of.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “παραγγελίαι” 7; ἐν β. ἡ ἀλήθεια 3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 117; εἴς τινα β. φλυαρίας ἐμπεσών 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 130d; ἀθεότητος 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.757c}}; ὑπέρκοσμος β. **abyss**, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th-6th c.AD: Damascius Philosophus “de Principiis” 106, 205.}}

1039:
** βύσσινος**, η, ον,
**made of** βύσσος, σινδὼν β. **fine linen** bandage, used for mummy-cloths, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.86; for wounds, [prev. author] 7.181; πέπλοι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 125 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Bacchae” 821; φάρος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 373; ὀθόνια β. “OGI” 90.17 (from Rosetta), “PStrassb.” 91.16 (1st c.BC), Aristeas 320; β. περιβόλαια “PStrassb.” 91.9 (1st c.BC)}}; βύσσινον, τό, {{LXX+NT|LXX.Est.1.6 (_plural_), +others, NT.Rev.19.8; ὀθόνια καὶ βύσσινα “PHolm.” 15.26.}}
**II** ={πορφυροῦς}, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

1041:
** βωμός**, ὁ,
(βαίνω) **raised platform, stand**, for chariots, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 8.441}}; **base** of a statue, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.100: but}}
**2** mostly, **altar with a base**, ἱερὸς β. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.305, etc.; πρὸς βωμῷ σφαγείς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 305; βωμὸς ἀρῆς φυγάσιν ῥῦμα [prev. author] “Supp.” 84 (Lyric poetry); βωμῶν ἀπείργειν τινά [prev. author] “Ch.” 293; ἀγυιεὺς β. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 370}}; of suppliants, ποτὶ βωμὸν ἵζεσθαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.334}}; βωμοῖσι προσῆσθαι, προσπεσόντα βωμῷ καθῆσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 16, “OC” 1158; βωμὸν ἵζειν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1314}}: also in Prose, β. ἱδρύσασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.142, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 322a; ἱζόμενοι ἐπὶ τὸν β. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.108; ἐπὶ βωμῶν καθέζεσθαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.11.}}
**3** later, **tomb, cairn,** {{Refs|epigram Gr. 319.}}
**4** title of poems by Dosiades and Besantinus, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 15.26and25, compare 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Lex.” 25.}}
**5** **altar-shaped cake,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Inscriptiones Graecae 2.1651B,C, 2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 6.76.}}
**6** Ζεὺς Βωμός, probably a Syrian god, {{Refs|“Hermes” 37.118 (from Syria).}}
**7** **central fire** in the system of Philolaus, _accusative_ to {{Refs|“Placita Philosophorum” 2.7.7.}}
**8** in _plural_, ={ἔμβολοι}, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

1047:
** γάζα** [γᾱ], ἡ,
**treasure**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 8.11.5, “OGI” 54.22(3rd c.BC), epigram cited in 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.1.39, LXX.2Esd.5.17, NT.Act.8.27, etc.}}; ἐκ τῆς βασιλικῆς γ. {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 17.35.}}
**II** **large sum of money**, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 11.34.12. (Persian word.)}}

1050:
** γάϊος**[ᾱ], α, ον,
_Doric dialect_for γήϊος, **onland**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 826 (Lyric poetry)}}; **earthy**, γ. κόνις {{Refs|[prev. author] “Th.” 736}}; **of the land**, κόγχοι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 42.9}}; παῖς γ. child **of earth, terrae filius**, of a slave, probably in {{Refs|“IG” 14.1432 (compare γάϊος παρὰ Ἰταλιώταις καὶ Ταραντίνοις ὁ μίσθιος 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 188.30, compare “Etymologicum Magnum” 223.24)}}; ἄνεμος a **land** wind, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}; also, ={ἐργάτης βοῦς}, {{Refs|[prev. author], “Etymologicum Magnum” [prev. cited]}}
**II** τὸν γάϊον, ={καταχθόνιον}, probably in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 156 (Lyric poetry).}}

1051:
** γάλα** [??], τό,
_genitive_ γάλακτος (also γάλακος, _dative_ γάλακι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hecale” 1.4.4, probably in 5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 108.18, compare “Anecdota Graeca - MSS. Bibl. Oxon.” 4.338), also τοῦ γάλα}} _indeclinable_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Comicus 238: _dative_ _plural_ γάλαξι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 887d. (For γλακτ-}}, cf. Latin _lac_ for **glact):—milk**, ἀμελγόμενοι γ. λευκόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.434, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.88, etc.}}; εὔποτον γ., εὐτραφὲς γ., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 611, “Ch.” 898}}; ἐν γάλακτι ὤν, τεθραμμένη, at **the breast**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1266, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 81c}}; ἐν γάλαξι τρέφεσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Lg.” [prev. cited] (so _metaphorically_, ἐν σπαργάνοις καὶ γάλαξιν εἶναι, of art, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “Varia Historia” 8.8); διδόναι γάλα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 7.4}}; ἐμπλῆσαι γάλακτος to fill full of **milk**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 24.3: _metaphorically_, οἶνος, Ἀφροδίτης γ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 596.}}
**2** ὀρνίθων γ. (ὄρνιθος γάλα, ={ὀρνιθόγαλον}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “fragment” 71.5, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 2.144), proverbial of rare and dainty things, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 508, “Av.” 734, Mcn. 936; τὸ λεγόμενον, σπανιώτατον πάρεστιν ὀρνίθων γ. 4th c.BC: Mnesimachus Comicus 9, cf. Ach. Tat. “Intr.Arat.” 4 (explained by 5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus 22}} as **white of egg**, compare {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Scholia 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc.Cond.” 13).}}
**3** ἀγαθὸν γ. a good **wet-nurse**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Epigrammata” 51}}; οὐδ᾽ εἰ γ. λαγοῦ εἶχον.. καὶ ταὧς, κατήσθιον {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 123.}}
**II** **milky sap** of plants, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 6.3.4, etc.}}
**III** **the milky way**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 11, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 345a12, 4th-3rd c.BC: Aratus Epicus 476; but ὁ τοῦ γάλακτος κύκλος 3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra “Phaenomena” p.4M., 1st c.BC: Geminus Astronomicus 5.69.}}

1055:
** γᾰλήν-η**, ἡ,
**stillness of the sea, calm** (γ. μὲν ἐν θαλάσσῃ νηνεμία δ᾽ ἐν ἀέρι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 108b25, but compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 5.452, 12.168), 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus only in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”, λευκ ὴ δ᾽ ἦν ἀμφὶ γαλήνη 10.94}}; οἱ δ᾽ ἐλόωσι γαλήνην will sail the **calm sea**, {{Refs|7.319}}; **stillness** of deep waters, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.AD: Coluthus Epicus 360; νηνεμίας τε καὶ γ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 153c; ἐν ταῖς γ. καὶ εὐδίαις 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 533b30}}: _metaphorically_ of the mind, **calmness, serenity**, φρόνημα νηνέμου γαλάνας {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 740 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐν γαλήνῃ in **calm, quiet**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 899; γ. ἐν τῇ ψυχῇ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 791a.}}
**II** **lead sulphide, galena**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Plinius Rerum Naturalium Scriptor “Historia Naturalis” 33.95, 34.159.}}
**III** name of an **antidote**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Andromachus Poeta Medicus cited in 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 14.32. (_Aeolic dialect_ γελήνη (sic)}} _accusative_ to {{Refs 6th c.AD+|6th c.AD: Joannes Alexandrinus Grammaticus “Comp.” 3.1}}; perhaps akin to γελάω.)

1060:
** γᾰμέω**,
_future_ γαμέω {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.388, 391}}, _contraction_ γαμῶ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 764, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1500, “Ant.” 750, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Orestes” 1655, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.2.12, etc.; later γαμήσω 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.386c, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Rh.Pr.” 8 (for γαμήσεις “Tim.” 52 to be read γαμησείεις)}}: _aorist 1_ ἔγημα {{NT+8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.121, etc.; later ἐγάμησα LXX.Est.10.3, NT.Mark.6.17, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “D Deor.” 5.4, etc. (compare below 11.2):}}_perfect_ γεγάμηκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Lysistrata” 595, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 877e}}: _pluperfect_ ἐγεγαμήκει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.126}}:—_middle_, _future_ _Epic dialect_ γαμέσσεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.394}} codices, _Attic dialect_ γαμοῦμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1673, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 900, later γαμήσομαι 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Artoxerxes” 26, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἐγημάμην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.392, 6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 86, 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 5.5, etc.}}:—_passive_, _future_ γαμηθήσομαι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Josephus Historicus “Antiquitates Judaicae” 6.13.8, 2nd c.AD(?): Antoninus Liberalis Mythographus 1.2, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 58.3, 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 5.30, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἐγαμήθην {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 11.34, 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 10.4.20, etc.: poetry shortened γαμεθεῖσα variant in 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 8.91, compare 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 758.52}}: _perfect_ γεγάμημαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.5.24, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.32}}: _pluperfect_ ἐγεγάμητο {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Appianus Historicus “Bella Civilia” 4.23}}: (γάμος):—**marry**, i.e. **take to wife**, of the man, Ἀδρήστοιο δ᾽ ἔγημε θυγατρῶν one of his daughters, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.121; ἔνθα δ᾽ ἔγημε γυναῖκα 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.241}}; γ. γυναῖκα ἐς οἰκία, like{ἄγεσθαι}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.78: with _accusative_ _cognate_, γάμον γαμεῖν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 764, 909; τὸν Ἑλένης γάμον.. γήμας 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 467}}; γῆμαι γέκτρα βασιλέως the king's daughter, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Med.” 594: rarely with double _accusative_, γάμους τοὺς πρώτους ἐγάμεε Κύρου δύο θυγατέρας (for πρῶτον ἐγάμεε.. θυγατέρας) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.88, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 357}}; also γάμῳ γ. **marry** in lawful wedlock, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 39.26}}; ἐκ κακοῦ, ἐξ ἀγαθοῦ γῆμαι, **marry a wife** of mean or noble stock, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 189, 190; ἐκ μειόνων 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Hiero” 1.28; ἐκ γενναίων 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 1279}}; παρά τινος{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 975, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 310c}}; ἐπὶ θυγατρὶ γ. ἄλλην γυναῖκα set a stepmother over one's daughter, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.154, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 372}}; ἐπὶ δέκα ταλάντοις τινὰ γαμεῖν **marry a wife** with a dowry of ten talents, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 4.13.}}
**2** of mere sexual intercourse, **take for a paramour**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.36, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 32; γ. βιαίως σκότιον λέχος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 44.}}
**3** later of the woman, ἐὰν γαμήσῃ ἄλλον {{NT|NT.Mark.10.12: _absolutely_, NT.1Cor.7.28.}}
**II** _middle_, **give oneself** or **one's** child **in marriage**:
**II.1** of the woman, **give herself in marriage**, i.e. **wed**, with _dative_, γαμέεσθαι τῷ ὅτεῴ τε πατὴρ κέλεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.113; γημαμένη ᾧ υἷϊ· ὁ δ᾽ ὃν πατέρ᾽ ἐξεναρίξας γῆμεν 11.273: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.117; σοὶ μὲν γαμεῖσθαι μόρσιμον, γαμεῖν δ᾽ ἐμοί 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 13}}; εἰς τύρανν᾽ ἐγημάμην I **married** into a royal house, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 474}}; γήματο δ᾽ εἰς Μαραθῶνα, i. e. she **married** Herodes of Marathon, [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|“IG” 14.1389 i 5 (ἥν τ᾽ ἐγήματο is _falsa lectio_ for{ἥ τ᾽ ἐγ}. in 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 262)}}]: ironically of a henpecked husband, κεῖνος οὐκ ἔγημεν ἀλλ᾽ ἐγήματο {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Anacreon Lyricus 86}}; so Medea to Jason, μῶν γαμοῦσα.. σ; did I **marry** you? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 606}}; ἐγημάμην, of a man marrying a rich wife, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 46}}; γαμεῖται ἕκαστος (obscene meaning) {{Refs 1st c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “VH” 1.22; ὁ γαμηθεὶς ὡς παρθένος κἄπειτα γενόμενος ἀνήρ 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 2}}; incorrectly, in later writers, γημάμενος {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Apollodorus Damascenus Mechanicus 3.12.6, compare 4th c.AD(?): Quintus Smyrnaeus Epicus 1.728.}}
**II.2** of parents, **get their children married, betroth** them, **get a wife for the son**, Πηλεύς θήν μοι ἔπειτα γυναῖκα γαμέσσεται αὐτός (where {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Aristarchus Grammaticus γε μάσσεται}} **will seek** or **make suit** for) {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 9.394:}} —_active_ _aorist 1_ ἐγάμησα{{Refs 4th c.BC+|in this sense, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 885.}}
**III** _passive_, **to be taken to wife**: hence, **marry a husband**, [prev. cited] ad [near the start], {{Refs 4th c.AD+|“PGrenf.” 2.76.11 (4th c.AD)}}, etc.; rarely in correct authors, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 3.45.}}

1062:
** γάμος** [ᾰ], ὁ,
**wedding**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.4.29, +others; γάμοι εἰλαπίναι τε 18.491}}; γάμον τεύχειν furnish forth **a wedding**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.277; γ. δαινύναι 4.3}}; ἀρτύειν{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 770; γάμον ποιεῖν 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 7.86, “Test.Epict.” 2.19: _plural_, γάμους διττοὺς ἑστιᾶν 4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.9}}; of a single wedding, οἰκοσίτους τοὺς γ. ποιεῖσθαι {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 450; γάμους ποιεῖν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 30.21, NT.Matt.22.2; ἐπιτελεῖν γ. τῆς θυγατρός 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Fragmenta” 549, compare 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.84; οἱ κεκλημένοι εἰς τοὺς γ. 4th-3rd c.BC: Diphilus Comicus 17.2; ἐν τοῖς γ. ἄκλητος εἰσδεδυκέναι 4th-3rd c.BC: Apollodorus Carystius Comicus 24.}}
**II** **marriage, wedlock**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.382, etc.; ἄγειν [γυναῖκα] ἐπὶ γάμῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.4.8; ἀγαγέσθαι τινὰ πρὸς γάμον 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Cat.Ma.” 24}}; τὸν Οἰνέως γ. the **marriage** granted by O., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 792; γ. θεῶν τινος 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 979, compare “IT” 25; εἰς γ. τινὸς ἐλθεῖν [prev. author] “IA” 1044 (Lyric poetry); more frequently in _plural_, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 558 (Lyric poetry), 739 (Lyric poetry), “Ag.” 1156 (Lyric poetry), etc.; compare γαμέω 1: also τοῖς μεθημερινοῖς γάμοις}}, i.e. **prostitution**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.129}}; Πανὸς ἀναβοᾷ γάμους, i.e. **rape**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 190 (Lyric poetry)}}; of **unlawful wedlock**, as of Paris and Helen, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tr.” 932; γάμοι ἄρρενες 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “VH” 1.22; γ. ἀνδρεῖοι 6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “Historia Arcana” 16.23:— 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 103, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.4.19}}, do not establish the sense of **a wife**; for [{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 357, see at {γαμέω} I.1.}}]
**III** ἱερὸς γ. **ritual marriage**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 320, 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus, “Etymologicum Magnum” 468.56; as a nickname, 4th c.BC: Anaxandrides Comicus 34.2}}; name of play by Ale.Comedy texts
**IV** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Pythagoras Philosophus}} name for **three,** {{Refs|Theol.Ar. 16}}; for **five**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.388c; for six, Theo Sm.p.102H., “Theologumena Arithmeticae” 33.}}
**V** Γάμος personified, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Philoxenus Epigrammaticus 13, 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 5.27.}}
**VI** name of month at Epidaurus, {{Refs|“IG” 4.1485,1492}}. (Perh. akin to Sanskrit _jāmís_ 'brother or sister', Latin _geminus._)

1063:
** γάρ**
(γε, ἄρα), causal _conjunction_, used alone or with other Particles.
**I** introducing the reason or cause of what precedes, **for**, τῷ γὰρ ἐπὶ φρεσὶ θῆκε θεὰ λευκώλενος Ἥρη· κήδετο γ. Δαναῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.56, etc.}}; but frequently in explanation of that which is implied in the preceding clause, πολλάων πολίων κατέλυσε κάρηνα.. τοῦ γὰρ κράτος ἐστὶ μέγιστον {{Refs|2.118, etc.: hence}}
**I.b** in simple explanations, especially after a Pronoun or _demonstrative_ _adjective_, ἀλλὰ τόδ᾽ αἰνὸν ἄχος κραδίην καὶ θυμὸν ἱκάνει· Ἕκτωρ γ. ποτε φήσει {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8.148, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.163}}; ὃ δὲ δεινότατον.. ὁ Ζεὺς γ... {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 514; ὃ δὲ πάντων ἀδικώτατον ἔδοξε· τῶν γὰρ προγε γραμμένων ἠτίμωσε καὶ υἱούς 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Sulla” 31}}; frequently in introducing proofs or examples, μαρτύριον δέ· Δήλου γ. καθαιρομένης.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.8}}; τεκμήριον δέ· οὔτε γ. Λακεδαιμόνιοι.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.39, compare 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.10, etc.}}; in full, τεκμήριον δὲ τούτου τόδε· αἱ μὲν γ... {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.58}}; παράδειγμα τόδε τοῦ λόγου· ἐκ γ... {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.2; δηλοῖ δέ μοι τόδε· πρὸ γ... [prev. work] 3.}}
**I.c** to introduce a detailed description or narration already alluded to, ὅμως δὲ λεκτέα ἃ γιγνώσκω· ἔχει γ. [ἡ χώρα] πεδία κάλλιστα.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.6, etc.}}
**I.d** in answers to questions or statements challenging assent or denial, **yes,.., no,.. **, οὔκουν.. ἀνάγκη ἐστ;—ἀνάγκη γ. οὖν, ἔφη, **ay doubtless** it is necessary, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.1.7, compare § 4 and 13; indicating assent, ἔχει γ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 268a}}; ἱκανὸς γ., ἔφη, συμβαίνει γ., ἔφη, {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 502b, 502c,compare “Ap.” 41a, etc.}}; οὔκουν δὴ τό γ᾽ εἰκός.—οὐ γ.: {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 276c.}}
**I.2** by inversion, preceding the fact explained, **since, as**, Ἀτρεΐδη, πολλοὶ γ. τεθνᾶσιν Ἀχαιοί.. τῷ σε χρὴ πόλεμον παῦσαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.328}}; χρόνου δὲ οὐ πολλοῦ διελθόντος (χρῆν γ. Κανδαύλῃ γενέσθαι κακῶς) ἔλεγε πρὸς τὸν Γύγην τοιάδε, Γύγη, οὐ γ. σε δοκέω πείθεσθαι.. (ὦτα γ. τυγχάνει κτλ.), ποίει ὅκως.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8. compare 6.102, +others}}; εἶεν, σὺ γ. τούτων ἐπιστήμων, τί χρὴ ποιεῖ; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 117a}}; the principal proposition is sometimes
**I.2.b** blended with the causal one, τῇ δὲ κακῶς γ. ἔδεε γενέσθαι εἶπε, i.e. ἡ δέ (κακῶς γ. οἱ ἔδεε γενέσθαι) εἶπε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.109, compare 1.24, 4.149, 200, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.72, 8.30.}}
**I.2.c** attached to the hypothet. Particle instead of being joined to the apodosis, οὐδ᾽ εἰ γ. ἦν τὸ πρᾶγμα μὴ θεήλατον, ἀκάθαρτον ὑμᾶς εἰκὸς ἦν οὕτως ἐᾶν, i.e. οὐδὲ γ. εἰ ἦν.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 255.}}
**I.2.d** repeated, οὐ γ. οὖν σιγήσομαι· ἔτικτε γ... {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 980, compare “Ant.” 659f, 1255.}}
**I.3** in elliptical phrases, where that of which γάρ gives the reason is omitted, and must be supplied,
**I.3.a** frequently in Trag. dialogue and {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus}}, when **yes** or **no** may be supplied from the context, καὶ δῆτ᾽ ἐτόλμας τούσδ᾽ ὑπερβαίνειν νόμου;—οὐ γ. τί μοι Ζεὺς ἦν ὁ κηρύξας τάδε [yes], **for** it was not Zeus, etc., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 450, compare “OT” 102, etc.; καλῶς γὰρ αὐτὸς ἠγάνισαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 194a}}; frequently in phrase ἔστι γ. οὕτω [yes], **for** so it is, i. e. yes certainly: λέγεταί τι καινό; γένοιτο γ. ἄν τι καινότερον ἢ..; [why,] could there be.. ? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.10; with negatives, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 262 τούτῳ γ. οὐ νικήσετε}} [do so], **yet** shall ye never prevail by this means: for ἀλλὰ γ., see below{{Refs|11.1.}}
**I.3.b** to confirm or strengthen something said, οἵδ᾽ οὐκέτ᾽ εἰσί· τοῦτο γάρ σε δήξεται [I say this], **for** it will sting thee, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1370}}: after an Exclamation, ὦ πόποι· ἀνάριθμα γ. φέρω πήματα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 168 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 857.}}
**I.3.c** in conditional propositions, where the condition is omitted, **else**, οὐ γ. ἄν με ἔπεμπον πάλιν (i.e. εἰ μὴ ἐπίστευον) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.6.33}}; γίνεται γ. ἡ κοινωνία συμμαχία **for in that case**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1280b8.}}
**I.4** in abrupt questions, **why, what**, τίς γ. σε θεῶν ἐμοὶ ἄγγελον ἧκε; **why** who hath sent thee? {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.182; πῶς γ. νῦν.. εὕδουσ; 10.424}}; πατροκτονοῦσα γ. ξυνοικήσεις ἐμο; **what**, wilt thou.. ? {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 909: generally, after _interrogative_ Particles, ἦ γ.}}..; **what**, was it.. ? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1000, 1039, etc.}}; τί γ.; **quid enim**? i. e. it must be so, {{Refs|[prev. author] “OC” 539, 542, 547, etc.}}; τί γ. δή ποτ; {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.44}}; also πῶς γ.; πῶς γ. ο;, see at {πῶς}.
**I.5** to strengthen a wish, with _optative_, κακῶς γ. ἐξόλοιο O that you might perish ! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 261}}; compare αἴ, εἰ, εἴθε, πῶς.
**II** joined with other Particles:
**II.1** ἀλλὰ γ. where γάρ gives the reason of a clause to be supplied between ἀλλά and itself, as ἀλλ᾽ ἐν γὰρ Τρώων πεδίῳ.. but [**far otherwise], for**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.739}}; ἀλλὰ γὰρ ἥκουσ᾽ αἵδ᾽ ἐπὶ πρᾶγος πικρόν but [**hush], for**.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 861}}; ἀλλ᾽ οὐ γ. σ᾽ ἐθέλω.. but [**look out] for**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.242; in full, ἀλλ᾽ οὐ γάρ σφιν ἐφαίνετο κέρδιον εἶναι μαίεσθαι προτέρω, τοὶ μὲν πάλιν αὖτις ἔβαινον 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.355; ἀλλ᾽, οὐ γ. ἔπειθε, διδοῖ τὸ φᾶρος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.109.}}
**II.2** γ. ἄρα **for** indeed, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 315d, “Smp.” 205b.}}
**II.3** γ. δή **for** of course, **for** you know, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.301, 23.607, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.34, 114, etc.}}; φάμεν γ. δή **yes** certainly we say so, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 187e, compare 164d; οὐ γ. δή 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 46, etc.}}
**II.4** γ. νυ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.359.}}
**II.5** γ. οὖν **for** indeed, to confirm or explain, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.232, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.34, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 489, 771, etc.}}; φησὶ γ. οὖν **yes** of course he says so, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 170a; γ. οὖν δή [prev. author] “Prm.” 148c, etc.; οὐ γ. οὖν [prev. work] 134b}}; compare τοιγαροῦν.
**II.6** γ. που **for** I suppose, especially with _negatives_, {{Refs|[prev. author] “R.” 381c, “Phd.” 62d, etc.; οὐ γ. δήπου [prev. author] “Prt.” 309c.}}
**II.7** γ. ῥα, ={γὰρ ἄρα}, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.113, +others}}
**II.8** γ. τε, {{Refs|23.156}}; also τε γ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.159, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1333a2, +others}}
**II.9** γ. τοι **for** surely.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Helena” 93, “Supp.” 564, etc.; οὐ γ. τοι 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.172, etc.}}; compare τοιγάρτοι.
**B** POSITION: γάρ properly stands after the first word in a clause, but in Pocts it frequently stands third or fourth, when the preceding words are closely connected, as ὁ μὲν γὰρ.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 764}}; χἠ ναῦς γὰρ.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 527}}; τό τ᾽ εἰκαθεῖν γὰρ.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ant.” 1096}}; τὸ μὴ θέμις γὰρ.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 641, compare 753: also in Prose, τὸ κατ᾽ ἀξίαν γὰρ.. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1163b11}}: sometimes for metrical reasons, where there is no such connexion, as third {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 222.729, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 219 (all Lyric poetry)), fourth (5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 1545)}}; in later Comedy texts fifth {{Refs 4th c.BC+|(4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 462.2); sixth (4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 26.22); seventh (4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 531, “Pk.” 170, 3rd c.BC: Athenio Comicus 1.5)}}; once sixth in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus, καιρὸς καὶ πλοῦς ὅδ᾽ ἐπείγει γὰρ κατὰ πρύμναν “Ph.” 1451.}}
**B.2** inserted before the _demonstrative_ -ί, as νυνγαρί for νυνὶ γά; compare νυνί.
**C** QUANTITY: γάρ is sometimes long in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus for the sake of meter, θήσειν γὰρ ἔτ᾽ ἔμελλεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.39; φωνῆς γὰρ ἤκουσα “hymnus ad Cererem” 57}}.—In _Attic dialect_ always short: {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 366, “V.” 217, “Lys.” 20 are corrupt.}}

1064:
** γαστήρ**, ἡ,
_genitive_ έρος, γαστρός: _dative_ -έρι, γαστρί (the longer forms in _Epic dialect_, Lyric poetry, and once in Trag., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 220)}}: _dative_ _plural_ γαστῆρσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ νούσων” 4.54, γαστράσι 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 54.22}}:—**paunch, belly**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.372, etc.}}; γ. ἀσπίδος **the hollow** of a shield, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 11.24}}; **belly** or **wide part** of a bottle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Cratinus Comicus 190.}}
**2** **the belly**, as craving food, κέλεται δέ ἑ γ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 6.133; βόσκειν ἣν γαστέρ᾽ 17.228}}; γαστέρι δ᾽ οὔ πως ἔστι νέκυν πενθῆσαι, i. e. by fasting, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.225; ἐν γαστρὸς ἀνάγκαις 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 726 (Lyric poetry)}}; to express **gluttony**, γαστέρες οἶον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 26; γ. ἀργαί 6th c.BC: Epimenides Philosophus 1; ἐγκράτεια γαστρὸς καὶ ποτοῦ 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.8, compare “Oec.” 9.11}}; γαστρὸς ἐγκρατής master of **his belly**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Mem.” 1.2.1; opposed to γαστρὸς ἥττων, [prev. work] 1.5.1}}; γαστρὶ δουλεύειν, χαρίζεσθαι, to be the slave of **his belly**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1.6.8, 2.1.2}}; γ. δελεάζεσθαι{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 2.1.4; τῇ γ. μετρεῖν τὴν εὐδαιμονίαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.296}}; τᾶς γαστρὸς φείδεσθαι, com. of one who has nothing to eat, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 21.41.}}
**3** **paunch stuffed with mincemeat, sausage, haggis**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.44, 118, 20.25, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 409.}}
**II** **womb**, ὅντινα γαστέρι μήτηρ.. φέροι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.58}}; ἐκ γαττρός from **the womb**, from **infancy**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 305}}; ἐν γαστρὶ ἔχουσα big with child, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.32; ὗν ἔχουσαν ἐν γ. “PFlor.” 130.3 (3rd c.AD)}}; ἐν γ. φέρουσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 792e}}; ἐν γ. λαβεῖν **conceive**, {{LXX+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 632a28, “Anthologia Graeca” 11.18 (1st c.AD: Nicarchus Epigrammaticus), LXX.Gen.30.41, +others}}; συλλαμβάνειν variant {{LXX+NT+5th c.BC+|LXX.Gen.25.21, cf. NT.Luke.1.31; ἐς γ. βάλλεσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.28}}; κατὰ γαστρὸς ἔχειν Vett.{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|Val. 193.33; φέρειν “Geoponica” 16.1.3; also γυνὴ ἑπτὰ ἤδη γαστέρας δυστοκοῦσα 2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Vita Apollonii” 3.39. (Perh.for γραστήρ, compare γράω.)}}

1065:
** γε**,
_Doric dialect_ and _Boeotian dialect_ **γα**, enclitic Particle, giving emphasis to the word or words which it follows.
**I** with single words, **at least, at any rate**, but often only to be rendered by italics in writing, or emphasis in pronunciation: τὸ γὰρ.. σιδήρου γε κράτος ἐστίν such is the power of **iron**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.393}}; εἴ που πτωχῶν γε θεοὶ.. εἰσίν if the poor **have** any gods to care for them,{{Refs|17.475; μάλιστά γε 4.366}}; ὅ γ᾽ ἐνθάδε λεώς **at any rate** the people here, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 42, etc.: with negatives, οὐ δύο γε}} not **even** two, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.303, 20.286}}; οὔκουν φθόγγος γε not **the least** sound, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 9.}}
**I.2** with Pronouns: with _pronoun_ of _1st pers._ [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.AD: Perses Epigrammaticus so closely joined, that the accent is changed, in ἔγωγε, ἔμοιγε (also ἔγωγα _Laconian dialect_, but ἐγώνγα, ἰώνγα _Boeotian dialect_): in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus frequently with _Article_ used as _pronoun_, see at {ὅ γε}: with _demonstrative_ Pronouns, κεῖνός γε, τοῦτό γε, etc.: in Comedy texts coalescing with -ί final, αὑτηγί 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 784}}]; τουτογί, ταυταγί, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] “V.” 781, “Pax” 1057, etc. (but ἐνγεταυθί “Th.” 646): after possess. Pronouns, ἐμόν γε θυμόν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.425, etc.: frequently after _relative_ Pronouns, ὅς γε, οἵ γε, etc., οἵ γέ σου καθύβρισαν 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1364; ὅς γ᾽ ἐξέλυσας δασμόν [prev. author] “OT” 35, etc.}}; ὅσον γε χρῄζεις **even** as much as..,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 365; οἷόν γέ μοι φαίνεται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 329a}}: rarely with _interrogative_ Pronouns, τίνα γε.. εἶπας **;**{{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Troades” 241}}; ποίου γε τούτου πλήν γ᾽ Ὀδυσσέως ἐρεῖς **;** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 441.}}
**I.3** after Conjunctions, to emphasize the modification or condition introduced by the subjoined clause, πρίν γε, before **at least**, sometimes repeated, οὐ μὲν.. ὀΐω πρίν γ᾽ ἀποπαύσεσθαι, πρίν γε.. αἵματος ἆσαι Ἀρῆα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.288, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.127}}; πρὶν ἄν γε or πρίν γ᾽ ἄν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 961, “Ra.” 78, etc.; ὅτε γε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 84e; ὁπότε γε 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 1699; ἐπεί γε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.3.9; ἐπειδή γε 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.18; ὅπου γε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.11}}; εἴ γε, ἐάν γε, if **that is to say**, if **really**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.18, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 252c}}; also simply to lay stress on the condition, κἄν γε μὴ λέγω and **if** I do not.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 317}}; εἴπερ γε if **at any rate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.16.γ, 143}}, etc.; ὥστε γε (variant{ὥς γε}), with _infinitive_, so far **at least** as to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 230b; ὥς γ᾽ ἐμοὶ χρῆσθαι κριτῇ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 801}}; ὥς γε or ὥσπερ γε as **at least**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 570, “OT” 715, etc.}}:—γε may follow τε, when τε is closely attached to the preceding word, ὡς οἷόν τέ γε μάλιστα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.5.2, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 412b; ἐάντε γε [prev. author] “Plt.” 293d; οἵ τέ γε [prev. author] “Grg.” 454e:—for its use in opposed or disjunctive clauses, see below 11.3.}}
**I.4** after other Particles, καὶ μὴν.. γε, οὐ μὴν.. γε, with words intervening, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.4.12, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 518, etc.}}; after ἄν in apodosi, when preceded by οὐ or καί, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Ph.” 1215, “Or.” 784}}; ἄταρ.. γε but **yet**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 448}}; καίτοι γε, see at {καί το; ἀλλά γε} (without intervening words) is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 287b (to be read ἀλλ᾽ ἄγε), “R.” 331b (ἀλλά γε ἕν codices, ἀλλὰ ἕν γε 5th c.AD: Stobaeus, Joannes)}}; ἀλλά γε δή uncertain in {{Refs 1st c.AD+|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 262a; later, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.394c, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 10.49 codices: but}}
**I.5** when preceding other Particles, γε commonly refers to the preceding word, while the Particle retains its own force: but sometimes modifies the sense of the following Particle, γε μήν **nevertheless**, πάντως γε μήν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 232, compare 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 754, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ and _Ionic dialect_ γε μέν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.703, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.195, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.152; γε μὲν δή 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 661, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 484; γε μέντοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 164a, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 2.3.9, etc.: γε δή}} frequently strengthens an assertion, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 42, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.62, etc.; οἰόμεθά γε δή 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthydemus” 275a (compare also 11.1)}}; γέ τοι, implying that the assertion is **the least** that one can say, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 934, “Pl.” 424, 1041, etc.; γέ τοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 447b; γέ τοι δή 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1171, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 264b; γέ τοί που [prev. author] “Lg.” 888e; γε δήπου [prev. author] “Phd.” 94a, etc.}}; γέ που **at all events, any how**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 896, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 607d, 478a, etc.}}; for γε οὖν, see at {γοῦν}.
**II** exercising an influence over the whole clause:
**II.1** epexegetic, **namely, that is**, Διός γε διδόντος **that is** if God grant it, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.390}}; κλῦθι, Ποσείδαον.., εἰ ἐτεόν γε σός εἰμι if **indeed** I am really thine, {{Refs|9.529: hence to limit, strengthen or amplify a general assertion, ἀνὴρ.. ὅστις πινυτός γε}} any man—**at least** any wise man, {{Refs|1.229}}; frequently preceded by καί, usually with words intervening, ἦ μὴν κελεύσω κἀπιθωΰξω γε πρός **ay and** besides that.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 73}}; παρῆσάν τινες καὶ πολλοί γε some, **ay and** a great many, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 58d; καὶ γελοίως γε [prev. author] “R.” 531a}}; frequently with the last term in an enumeration, ταύτῃ ἄρα.. πρακτέον καὶ γυμναστέον καὶ ἐδεστέον γε καὶ ποτέον {{LXX|[prev. author] “Cri.” 47b; ὄψεις τε καὶ ἀκοαὶ καὶ.. καὶ ἡδοναί γε δή [prev. author] “Tht.” 156b; repeated, συνήγαγόν μοι καί γε ἀργύριον καί γε χρυσίον LXX.Eccl.2.8}}; rarely without intervening words, καί γε ὁ θάνατος διὰ τὴν μοίρην ἔλαχεν {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ ἑπταμήνου” 9, compare 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 11.7 codices; καί γε.. ἐκχεῶ NT.Act.2.18: hence}}
**II.2** in dialogue, in answers where something is added to the statement of the previous speaker, as ἔπεμψέ τίς σοι.. κρέα**;** Answ. καλῶς γε ποιῶν **yes and** quite right too, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 1049}}; κενὸν τόδ᾽ ἄγγος, ἢ στέγει τι**;** Answ. σά γ᾽ ἔνδυτα.. **yes indeed**, your clothes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1412}}; οὕτω γὰρ ἂν μάλιστα δηχθείη πόσις. Answ. σὺ δ᾽ ἂν γένοιο γ᾽ ἀθλιωτάτη γυνή **yes truly**, and you.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Med.” 817, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 680, etc.}}; πάνυ γε **yes** certainly, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 8e, etc.}}; οὕτω γέ πως **yes** somehowso, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tht.” 165c}}; sometimes preceded by καί, καὶ οὐδέν γ᾽ ἄτοπον **yes** and no wonder, {{Refs|[prev. work] 142b, compare d, 147e}}; sometimes ironically, εὖ γε κηδεύεις πόλιν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1212.}}
**II.3** to heighten a contrast or opposition,
**II.3.a** after conditional clauses, εἰ μὲν δὴ σύ γ᾽.., τῷ κε Ποσειδάων γε.. if **you** do so, then **at all events** Poseidon will.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.49}} {γέα}; ἐπεὶ πρὸς τοῦτο σιωπᾶν ἥδιόν σοι.. τόδε γε εἰπέ **at any rate** tell me this, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.5.20; εἰ μὴ τὸ ὅλον, μέρος γ᾽ ἐπιβάλλει 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.272:—sometimes in the protasis, εἰ γὰρ μὴ ἑκόντες γε.. ἀλλ᾽ ἀέκοντας.. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.120.}}
**II.3.b** in disjunctive sentences to emphasize an alternative, ἤτοι κεῖνόν γε.. δεῖ ἀπόλλυσθαι ἢ σέ.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. author] 1.11; ἤτοι κρίνομέν γε ἢ ἐνθυμούμεθα ὀρθῶς τὰ πράγματα 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.40; πατὴρ δ᾽ ἐμός.. ζώει ὅ γ᾽ ἢ τέθνηκε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.131, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 10.504}}: also in the second clause, εἰπέ μοι, ἠὲ ἑκὼν ὑποδάμνασαι ἤ σέ γε λαοὶ ἐχθαίρουσι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.214, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.10.θ, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1098f}}
**II.4** in exclamations, etc., ὥς γε μή ποτ᾽ ὤφελον λαβεῖν uncertain in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 70, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 977, “Ph.” 1003, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 93, 836, etc.}}; in oaths, οὔτοι μὰ τὴν Δήμητρά γ᾽ variant in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 698; μὰ τὸν Ποσειδῶ γ᾽ οὐδέποτ᾽ [prev. author] “Ec.” 748; καὶ ναὶ μὰ Δία γε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Apologia Socratis” 20; καὶ νὴ Δία γε 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1350, 1st-2nd c.AD: Dio Chrysostomus Sophista 17.4, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Merc.Cond.” 28, 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 11.59, etc.: with words intervening, καὶ νὴ Δί᾽, ὦ ἄνδρες Ἀθηναῖοι, ἕτεροί γε.. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 13.16; νὴ Δία, ὦ Ἀθηναῖοι, ὥρα γε ὑμῖν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.1.37}}; merely in strong assertions, τίς ἂν φιλέοντι μάχοιτ; ἄφρων δὴ κεῖνός γέ.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.209, etc.}}
**II.5** implying concession, εἶμί γε **well then** I will go (in apodosi), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 861; δρᾶ γ᾽ εἴ τι δράσεις [prev. author] “IA” 817, compare “Andr.” 239.}}
**III** γε frequently repeated in protasis and apodosis, as πρίν γε.., πρίν γε, see{{Refs 5th c.BC+|above 1.3; εἰ μή γε.. τινὶ μείζονι, τῇ γε παρούσῃ ἀτιμίᾳ 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.29}}; even in the same clause, οὐδέν γ᾽ ἄλλο πλήν γε καρκίνους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Vespae” 1507, compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.187, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 554, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 335b, “Grg.” 502a.}}
**IV** POSITION: γε normally follows the word which it limits; but is frequently placed immediately after the Article, as ὅ γε πόλεμος {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.66, etc.}}; or the _preposition_, κατά γε τὸν σὸν λόγον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.15; ἔν γε ταῖς Θήβαις 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1380; or δέ, νῦν δέ γε 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 144e}}; τὸ δέ γε{{Refs|[prev. work] 164b; δοῖμεν δέ γέ που ἄν [prev. author] “R.” 607d, compare “Phd.” 94a, etc.}}; frequently in retorts, ἁμές ποκ᾽ ἦμες ἄλκιμοι νεανίαι. Answ. ἁμὲς δέ γ᾽ εἰμές {{Refs|“Carmina Popularia” 18}}; οὐκ οἶδ᾽ ὅτι λέγεις. Answ. ἡ γραῦς δέ γε οἶδ᾽, ὡς ἐγῷμαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Ἐπιτρέποντες” 577, compare 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 1031, etc.}}

1069:
** γείτων**, ονος, ὁ, ἡ,
**neighbour, borderer**, γείτονες ἠδὲ ἔται Μενελάου {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.16, compare 9.48, 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 346, etc.}}; opposed to σύνοικος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 696b; γ. τινός 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 1451, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.2.4; τινί [prev. work] 2.3.18}}; ἐκ τῶν γ. or ἐκ γειτόνων **from** or **in the neighbourhood**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Plutus” 435, etc.; οἷον ἐκ γ. φωνὴν θηρευόμενοι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 531a; λύχνον ἐκ τῶν γ. ἐνάψασθαι 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 1.14; ἐκ γ. τῆς πατρίδος μετοικεῖν 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 21, compare 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 10.4.12; rarely ἀπὸ γ. 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.84}}; ἐν γειτόνων (i.e. οἴκοις) οἰκεῖν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 27, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Philops.” 25, etc.; τὸ χωρίον τὸ ἐν γ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 53.10: _metaphorically_, ἐν γ. εἶναι}} to be **of like kind**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Icar.” 8: proverbial, μέγα γείτονι γείτων 7th c.BC: Alcman Lyricus 50, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.87.}}
**II** as _adjective_, **neighbouring, bordering**, πόλις, πόντος, {{Refs|[prev. author] “P.” 1.32, “N.” 9.43}}; χώρα, πύλαι, ῥοαί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 67 (Lyric poetry), “Th.” 486, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 418 (Lyric poetry): with _dative_, Ἀθήναις γ. πόλις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 294; νεκροῖσι γ. θᾶκοι [prev. author] “HF” 1097}}; also in Prose, ἡ γ. πόλις {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 877b; οἱ γ. βάρβαροι 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.72c: _neuter_ γεῖτον 5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus: _neuter_ _plural_ γείτω “IG” 2.814a B 36.}}
**III** **γίτονας** (sic)· τὰ δύο αἰδοῖα, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}

1070:
** γελάω**,
_Epic dialect_ **γελόω** {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.105}}, _Aeolic dialect_ **γέλαιμι** {{Refs|Hdn.Gr. 2.463, +others}}; _Epic dialect_ _participle_ γελόωντες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.40, γελώοντες, -ώωντες, or -οίωντες [prev. work] 111, compare 20.390}}; _Epic dialect_ _imperfect_ γελώων or **-οίων**{{Refs|20.347}}; _Doric dialect_ _participle_ γελᾶσα, _3rd.pers._ _plural_ γελᾶντι, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 1.36, 90}}; _Aeolic dialect_ γελαίσας {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 2.5}}: _Attic dialect_ _future_ γελάσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 252b, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 1.16, etc.; later γελάσω “Anthologia Graeca” 2.178 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus), 11.29 (1st c.AD: Automedon Epigrammaticus), “Anacreontea” 38.8, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἐγέλᾰσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Taurica” 276, etc.}}; _Epic dialect_ ἐγέλασσα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.101}}; _Doric dialect_ ἐγέλαξα {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 20. ??, variant [prev. work] 7.42}}; _3rd.pers._ _plural_ γέλαν for ἐγέλασαν poetical cited in {{Refs|“Etymologicum Magnum” 255.6}}:—_passive_, _future_ **-ασθήσομαι**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Diogenes Laertius 1.78, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 2}}: _aorist_ ἐγελάσθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.10, (κατ-) 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.83, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 3c, etc.:}}_perfect_ γεγέλασται {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|(κατα-) 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “DMort.” 1.1.}}
**I** _absolutely_, **laugh**, ἁπαλὸν γελάσαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.465; ἀχρεῖον γ. 18.163; γναθμοῖσι γελοίων ἀλλοτρίοισιν 20.347; δακρυόεν γ. 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.484; μηδὲν ἵλεων γ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1011}}; ἡ δ᾽ ἐγέλασσε χείλεσιν, of feigned laughter, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.101}}; ἐγέλασσε δέ οἱ φίλον ἦτορ his heart **laughed** within him,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|21.389; γελῶ ὁρῶν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.36:}} —_passive_, εἵνεκα τοῦ γελασθῆι αι for the sake of **a laugh being raised**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 2.19.}}
**I.2** of things, γέλασσε δὲ πᾶσα περὶ χθών {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.362; γαῖά τε πᾶσ᾽ ἐγέλασσε “hymnus ad Cererem” 14; γελᾷ δέ τε δό ματα πατρός 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 40; γελῶντα ὑδάτη “Lyrica Alexandrina Adespota” 32.4.}}
**II** **laugh** at, ἐπ᾽ αὐτῷ ἡδὺ γέλασσαν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.270, 23.784; ἐπ᾽ ἀλλήλοισι γελῶσιν 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1113}}; γελᾷ δὲ δαίμων ἐπ᾽ ἀνδρὶ θερμῷ **laughs scornfully** at.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 560 (Lyric poetry)}}; ἐπί τινι at a thing, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.2.5, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 50a: frequently with _dative_, γελᾷ δὲ τοῖσδε.. ἄχεσιν πολὺν γέλωτα 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 957 (Lyric poetry), compare 1043, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 560}}; ἐγέλασα ψολοκομπίαις **was amused** at them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Eq.” 696; ὅταν ποτ᾽ ἀνθρώποισιν ἡ τύχη γελᾷ 4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 110; εἰς ἐχθροὺς γ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 79; ἐν κακοῖσι τοῖς ἐμοῖς 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 222: rarely with _genitive_ _person_, γελᾷ μου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 1125 (Lyric poetry), compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 175b, 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Dem.Enc.” 16, 6th c.AD: Procopius Caesariensis Historicus “de Bello Gothico” 4.28 (variant).}}
**II.2** with _accusative_, **deride**, τινά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 20.1; ἢ τόδε γελᾶτε, εἰ..; 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Symposium” 2.19}}; τί δὲ τοῦτ᾽ ἐγέλασας ἐτεόν**;** what is this you **are laughing at**? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 820}}:—_passive_, **to be derided**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 789 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 839 (Lyric poetry); πρός τινος [prev. author] “Ph.” 1023; παρά τινος [prev. author] “OC” 1423.}}

1071:
** γέλως**,
_Aeolic dialect_ **γέλος**, ὁ, _genitive_ γέλωτος, _Attic dialect_ γέλω: _dative_ γέλωτι, _Epic dialect_ γέλω or γέλῳ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.100: _accusative_ γέλωτα}}, _poetry_ (and late Prose, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 1.34.2, _falsa lectio_ in 4th c.AD: Palaephatus Paradoxographus 30) γέλων, see below (_accusative_ γέλω is variant in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.350, compare below): _genitive_ _plural_ γελώτων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 732c: _dative_ γέλωσιν 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.167, “PGiss.” 1.3.6 (2nd c.AD)}}: (γελάω):—**laughter**, γέλῳ ἔκθανον {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.100; γέλω.. παρέχουσαι 20.8}}; ἄσβεστον γέλω (variant{γέλον}) ὦρσεν{{Refs 8th c.BC+|[prev. work] 346; ἄσβεστος δ᾽ ᾰρ᾽ ἐνῶρτο γέλως.. θεοῖσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.599; γέλων δ᾽ ἑτάροισιν ἔτευχε 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.350; γέλων δ᾽ ἔθηκε συνδείπνοις 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 1172}}; γέλωτα ποιεῖν, μηχανᾶσθαι, κινεῖν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.11 and 14, “Smp.” 1.14; παρασκευάζειν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 669d}}; γέλων ξυντιθέναι, γέλωτα ἄγειν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 303, 382; γ. ἔχει τινά 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.344; γ. ἂν γίγνοιτο 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 295e; γέλωτος καταρραγέντος 2nd c.AD: Athenaeus Epigrammaticus 5.211c (so in _active_, πολλοὺς κατέρρηξεν ἡμῶν γέλωτας Hippoloch.[prev. work] 130c); κατασχεῖν γέλωτα 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.5, etc.; οὐ γέλωτα δεῖ σ᾽ ὀφλεῖν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 404, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Fragmenta” 898}}; ἐπὶ γέλωτι to provoke **laughter**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.82, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 405}}; γέλωτος ἄξια **ridiculous**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Heraclidae” 507}}; ἅμα or σὺν γέλωτι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 789d, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.2.18; μετὰ γέλωτος 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 144.6}}; ἐν γέλωτι προφέρειν in **joke**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.124d}}; πολὺς γ. loud **laughter**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.18, etc. (πλατὺς γ., which Thom.Mag. p.293 R. recommends, is not classical)}}; μέγιστος, ἰσχυρὸς γ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Plt.” [same place], “R.” 388c}}; Σαρδόνιος γ. (see. Σαρδόνιος); Αἰάντειος γ. a maniac's **laugh**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th-5th c.AD: Diogenianus Paroemiographus 1.17.}}
**2** _metaphorically_ of waves, ={γέλασμα}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Oppianus Anazarbensis Epicus “Halieutica - Poetae Bucolici et Didactici” 4.334.}}
**II** **occasion of laughter, food for laughter**, γ. γίγνομαί τινι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 902}}; ταῦτ᾽ οὐ γ. κλύειν ἐμο; {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Ion” 528; γέλωτά τινα τίθεσθαι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.29, 7.209; ἀποδεῖξαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 166a}}; εἰς γ. τρέπειν, ἐμβάλλειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.35, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.75; ἐν γέλωτι ποιεῖσθαί τι 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Hist.Conscr.” 32, etc.; γ. ἔσθ᾽ ὡς χρώμεθα τοῖς πράγμασι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.25; ὅσα γὰρ.., πλείων ἐστὶ γ. τοῦ μηδενός [prev. author] 14.26.}}
**III** **dimple** in the hinder parts, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Am.” 14.}}

1072:
** γεμ-ίζω**,
(γέμω) **fill full of, load, freight** or **charge with**, properly of a ship, τινός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.53, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.2.25, etc.}}; γεμίσας τὴν ναῦν ξύλων Test. cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.168; ναῦν σίτου 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.36; θηρίων τὰς ναῦς 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.18.8; τραπέζας θοίνης “OGI” 383.146 (from Commagene)}}; of animals, **load**, κτήνη {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PFay.” 117.14 (2nd c.AD),compare “PTeb.” 419.17 (3rd c.AD)}}: with _doubleaccusative_, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|“PFlor.” 195.4 (3rd c.AD)}}; σποδοῦ γ. λέβητας **charging** them **with** ashes, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 443}}; γεμίσω σε **let me fill** you, addressed to a cup, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Theopompus Comicus 32}}; αὑτόν **stuff, gorge**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Περικειρομένη” 296}}; τὴν κοιλίαν ἀπό τινος variant in {{NT|NT.Luke.15.16}}:—_middle_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.31; ἐγεμιζόμην ἀνθρωπείου τροφῆς 2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Asin.” 46}}:—_passive_, _metaphorically_ of the Cyclops, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 505 (Lyric poetry)}}; of bees, γεμισθεῖσαι ἀποπέτονται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 624b2: with _genitive_, γ. ἀλαζονείας, εὐσεβείας, 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.186, 357.}}
**II** later, with _accusative_ _of things_, γεμίζειν ὕδωρ (i.e. τὴν ὑδρίαν) **to fill** it **full of** water, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 3.13.3:}} —_passive_, οἶνον, πῦρ γεμισθείς, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|“Anthologia Graeca” 12.85 (1st c.BC: Meleager Epigrammaticus).}}

1073:
** γέμω**,
used only in _present_ and _imperfect_, **to be full**, properly of a ship, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.118, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.1.21: generally, πάντα γ. 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Misopogon” 368c.}}
**b** ={κύειν}, {{Refs 5th c.AD+|5th c.AD(?): Hesychius Legal icographus}}
**2** with _genitive_ _of things_, **to be full of**, πλοῖα γέμοντα χρημάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.25; λιμὴν ἔγεμεν πλοίων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Critias” 117e; κώμας πολλῶν καὶ ἀγαθῶν γεμούσας 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 4.6.27}}; of animals, **to be laden.** ὄνοι γέμοντες οἴνου καὶ βρωμάτων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd-1st c.BC: Posidonius Historicus 5: _metaphorically_, κόμπος τῆς ἀληθείας γ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 613, compare 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 4; γέμω κακῶν δή 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1245; γ. θρασύτητος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 649d; ἀσυμμετρίας καὶ αἰσχρότητος γέμουσα ψυχή [prev. author] “Grg.” 525a; πικρίας 1st c.BC: Philodemus Gadarensis Epigrammaticus “Ir.” p.56}} W.: with _dative_, **to be filled with**, ἰτρίοισι, πέμμασι, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Archippus Comicus 9, 4th c.BC: Antiphanes Comicus 174.2; γῆν πυρὸς γέμουσαν ῥεύμασιν 5th c.BC: Carcinus Tragicus 5; γ. ἐξ ἁρπαγῆς NT.Matt.23.25.}}

1074:
** γενεά**, ᾶς,
_Ionic dialect_ **γενεή**, ῆς, ἡ, _Epic dialect_ _dative_ γενεῆφι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.112}}: (γενέσθαι):
**I** of the persons in a family,
**I.1** **race, family**, Πριάμου γ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.306, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.222, 16.117; γενεήν τε τόκον τε 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 15.141; ἴδμεν.. γενεήν, ἴδμεν δὲ τοκῆας 20.203, compare 214}}; γενεῇ ὑπέρτερος higher **by birth** or **blood**, {{Refs|11.786 (but}}**younger** in {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus cited in Scholia ad l.)}}; ταύτης εἶναι γ. καὶ αἵματος **of** this **race** and blood, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.211; πατρόθεν ἐκ γενεῆς ὀνομάζειν 10.68}}; γενεῇ **by birthright**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.387}}; Αἰτωλὸς γενεήν **by descent**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.471; γενεὴν Διός 21.187}}; γενεὴ ἔκ τινος **descent** from..,{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 157; γενεὴν ἀπὸ Θρηίκης 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.134}}; of horses, **breed, stock**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.265, 268: _plural_, χρήματα καὶ γενεάς}} **families**, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Timoleon” 34}}; γενεὰν ποιεῖσθαι to have **issue**, [{{Refs|“GDI” 1798 (Delph.); πατριὰ καὶ γ., ={φρατρία καὶ γένος}, [prev. work]1152 (from Elis)}}]: hence, **tribe, nation**, Περσῶν γ., Τυρρηνῶν γ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 912 (Lyric poetry), “Eleg.” 2:—rare in Prose, τίς ὢν γενεά; 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.1.6; καὶ αὐτὸν καὶ τὰν γ. ἀπολέσθαι “SIG” 306.8 (Tegea, 4th c.BC).}}
**I.2** **race, generation**, οἵηπερ φύλλων γ. τοιήδε καὶ ἀνδρῶν [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.146; δύο γ. μερόπων ἀνθρώπων 1.250, etc., compare 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.142, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.14, 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus cited in 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.415e (but, ={μήν}, [prev. author] cited in 6th c.AD: Lydus, Joannes Laurentius Historicus “de Mensibus” 3.14); ἀστὴν ἐξ ἀστῶν ἀμφοτέρων ἐπὶ τρεῖς γ. γεγενημένην “SIG” 1015.6 (Halic.)}}]; **age**, γ. ἀνθρωπηΐη the historical, opposed to to the mythical, **age**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.122; ἐπὶ τῆς ἡμετέρας γ. 1st c.BC: Dionysius Halicarnassensis 3.15.}}
**I.3** **offspring**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.191, Oracle texts cited in 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.86, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 189 (Lyric poetry)}}; of a single person, Τυροῦς γ. (i. e. Pelias) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.136, comparel. 8(7).71.}}
**I.4** _metaphorically_, **class, kind**, τὸ σύμμετρον καὶ καλὸν καὶ ὁπόσα τῆς γ. ταύτης ἐστίν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 66b; ταύτης τοι γενεᾶς ὁ νοῦς οὗτος 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 5.1.7.}}
**II** of Time or Place,
**II.1** **birthplace**, γ. ἐπὶ λίμνῃ Γυγαίῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.390, compare 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 1.407}}; of an eagle's **evrie**, {{Refs|15.175.}}
**II.2** **age, time of life**, γενεῆφι νεώτατος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.112; γενεῇ πρεσβύτατος, προγενέστερος, ὁπλότερος, 6.24, 9.161, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.184.}}
**II.3** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **time of birth**, ἐκ γενεῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.33, 4.23; ἀπὸ γ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.8.}}

1077:
Included with: ** γενέσι-ος**, ον,
={γενέθλιος},{θεός} {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2. 402a}}; epithet of Posidon, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 2.38.4.}}
**II** Γενέσιον, τό, shrine of Posidon Γ., Paus. [prev. cited]
**III** γενέσια, τά, **day kept in memory of the birthday of the dead**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.26, cf. Ammon. p.36V., 2nd c.AD: Phrynichus Atticista 83}}; to be distinguished from γενέθλια **birthday-feast**, though used for it in {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 784d (assuming variant) and later Gk., “POxy.” 736.56 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD), “PFay.” 114.20 (1st-2nd c.AD), etc., 4th c.AD: Alciphro Epistolographus 3.18 and 55, NT.Matt.14.6, NT.Mark.6.21, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 47.18}}; so ἡ γ. ἡμέρα, ={ἡ γενέθλιος}, {{Refs|“CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2883c (from Branchidae)}}; ἡ γ. alone, {{Refs|“OGI” 583.14 (from Cyprus)}}; τῇ τοῦ Σεβαστοῦ ἐμμήνῳ γ. {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“IGRom.” 4.353b (Pergam., 2nd c.AD).}}

1078:
** γένεσις**, εως, ἡ,
(γενέσθαι) **origin, source**, Ὠκεανόν τε θεῶν γένεσιν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 14.201}}; Ὠκεανοῦ, ὅς περ γ. πάντεσσι τέτυκται{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 246, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 180d}}; **beginning**, in dual, τοῖν γενεσέοιν ἡ ἑτέρα {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phd.” 71e.}}
**II** **manner of birth**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.204, 6.69, etc.}}; **race, descent**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.146; πατρὸς οὖσα γένεσιν Εὐρύτου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 380}}; κατὰ γένεσιν, opposed to καθ᾽ υἱοθεσίαν, {{Refs|“IG” 12(1).181 (from Rhodes).}}
**II.2** Astrology texts, **nativity, geniture,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 11.164 (1st c.AD: Lucillius Epigrammaticus), 183 ([prev. author]), “epigram Gr.” 314.21 (from Smyrna), “PLond.” 1.98r60 (1st c.AD), 2nd c.AD: Vettius Valens Astrologus 216.6}}: hence, **lot, fortune**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Astrampsychus Onirocriticus “Oracula” 16.8, 23.7.}}
**III** **production, generation, coming into being**, opposed to ὄλεθρος, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.21}}; more usually opposed to φθορά, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 55a, etc.}}; περὶ γενέσεως καὶ φθορᾶς, title of work by {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus}}: generally, **formation**, πύου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἀφορισμοί” 2.47}}; **origination, making**, ἱματίων, περὶ τὰ ἀμφιέσματα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 281b, 3; γ. καὶ οὐσία δικαιοσύνης [prev. author] “R.” 359a.}}
**III.2** ={τὸ γίγνεσθαι}, **becoming**, opposed to οὐσία,{{Refs 5th c.AD+|[prev. work] 525b, “Ti.” 29c, 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “Inst.” 45, +others}}
**IV** concrete, **creation**, i.e. **all created things**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 245e; γ. καὶ κόσμος [prev. author] “Ti.” 29e, frequently in 1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus, as 1.3, +others, compare 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.3.2, etc.}}
**V** **race, kind** or **sort** of animals, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Politicus” 265b, etc.}}; **family**, δίδυμος γ. of the Spartan kings, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Lg.” 691d.}}
**VI** **generation, age**, {{Refs|[prev. author] “Phdr.” 252d: _plural_, [prev. author] “Plt.” 310d}}; κατὰ περίστασιν τῆς γ. according to the circumstances of his **time**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Porphyrius Tyrius Philosophus “Sententiae ad intelligibilia ducentes” 32.}}
**VII** παιδοπόρος γ. **genitalia muliebria,** {{Refs 1st c.AD+|Anthologia Graeca 9.311 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus).}}
**VIII** in Mathematics texts, **generation** of a figure, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD(?): Pappus Mathematicus 234.4, +others}}
**VIII.b** **origin** of a spiral, {{Refs|[prev. author] 272.7}}; ἡ ἐν γ. εὐθεῖα the **initial** line, {{Refs|[prev. author] 286.22.}}

1080:
** γενν-άω**,
_future_ _middle_ γεννήσομαι in _passive_ sense, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 19.2}} (but -ηθήσομαι{{Refs|[prev. author] 4.9)}}: (γέννα):—causal of γίγνομαι (compare γείνομαι), mostly of the father, **beget**, ὁ γεννήσας πατήρ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 1412}}; οἱ γεννήσαντές σε your **parents**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 2.1.27; τὸ γεννώμενον ἔκ τινος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.108, etc.}}; ὅθεν γεγενναμένοι **sprung**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 5.74}}; of the mother, **bring forth, bear**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 48, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Generatione Animalium” 716a22, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Respublica Lacedaemoniorum” 1.3, etc.}}:—_middle_, **produce from oneself, create**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 34b, “Mx.” 238a.}}
**2** **produce, grow, get**, κἂν σῶμα γεννήσῃ μέγα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 1077.}}
**3** _metaphorically_, **engender, produce**, λήθη τῶν ἰδίων κακῶν θρασύτητα γεννᾷ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus 196; παντοίαν ἀρετήν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 209e; διανοήματά τε καὶ δόξας [prev. author] “R.” 496a, etc.}}; γεννῶσι τὸν οὐρανὸν [οἱ φιλόσοφοι] **call** it **into existence**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “de Caelo” 283b31}}; ὁ ἐξ ἀσωμάτου γεννῶν λόγος{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|[prev. work] 305a16, compare 3rd c.AD: Plotinus Philosophus 6.6.9}}; of numbers, **produce** a total, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 1.347.}}

1081:
** γένν-ημα**, ατος, τό,
**that which is produced** or **born, child**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 315; παίδων τῶν σῶν νέατον γ. [prev. author] “Ant.” 627; τῶν Λαΐου.. τις ἦν γεννημάτων [prev. author] “OT” 1167}}: generally, any **product** or **work**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 597e, etc.}}: in _plural_, **fruits** of the earth, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.71.1, etc.; τῶν στοιχείων 1st c.BC: Philodemus Philosophus “περὶ σημείων καὶ σημειώσεων” 37.}}
**2** **breeding**, δηλοῖ τὸ γ. ὠμὸν (i.e. ὄν).. παιδός {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 471.}}
**II** _active_, **begetting**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 850 (_plural_, assuming variant).}}
**II.2** **producing**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 266d.}}
** γένημα**, ατος, τό,
**produce**, of the fruits of the earth (compare γέννημα), {{NT+3rd c.BC+|“PRev.Laws” 24.15, +others (3rd c.BC), LXX.Gen.40.17, +others; γ. τῆς ἀμπέλου NT.Mark.14.25: _plural_, τοῦ ἐνεστῶτος ἔτους “OGI” 262.9 (from Syria), compare “BGU” 188.9 (2nd c.AD), “POxy.” 277.6 (1st c.BC)}}; γ. καὶ ἐπιγενήματα {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PRyl.” 154.22 (1st c.AD)}}, etc. **γενημάτιον**, τό, _diminutive_ of {γενηΐς}, {{Refs|“PEdgar” 41.9.}}

1083:
** γένν-ησις**,
_Doric dialect_ **γένν-ᾱσις**, εως, ἡ, **engendering, producing**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 1065 (Lyric poetry, codices); γ. καὶ τόκος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 206e}}; **birth,** {{NT|Inscriptiones Graecae 22.1368.130, variant in NT.Luke.1.14.}}
**2** **production**, ἀγαθῶν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1332a18 (_plural_).}}

1084:
** γενν-ητός**, ή, όν,
**begotten**, υἱὸς γ., opposed to ποιητός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 923e}}; **mortal**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Icar.” 2}}; γεννητοὶ γυναικῶν **born of** women, {{NT|NT.Matt.11.11, NT.Luke.7.28.}}
**II** **generable**, opposed to φθαρτός, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 1027a29 (variant)}}; ὑλὴ γ. matter **for generation**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 1042b6.}} _adverb_ -τῶς **by means of generation**, {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD: Iamblichus Philosophus “de Mysteriis” 1.18.}}

1085:
** γένος**, εος
or ους, τό, **race, stock, kin**, ἀμφοτέροισιν ὁμὸν γ. ἠδ᾽ ἴα πάτρη {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.354; αἷμά τε καὶ γ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 8.583; ὑμετέρου δ᾽ οὐκ ἔστι γένεος βασιλεύτερον 15.533; γένος πατέρων αἰσχυνέμεν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.209; γ. ἀπόλωλε τοκήων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.62; ὅθι τοι γένος ἐστὶ καὶ αὐτῇ 6.35: frequently _absolutely_ in _accusative_, ἐξ Ἰθάκης γένος εἰμί}} from Ithaca I am **by race**,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|15.267, compare 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.544, 896, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 239, etc.}}; in _Attic dialect_ frequently with the _Article_, ποδαπὸς τὸ γένος εἶ **;** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 186, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 216a}}: so in _dative_, γένει πολῖται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 23.24}}; γένει υἱός, opposed to an adopted son, {{Refs|[prev. author] 44.2}}; οἱ ἐν γένει, ={συγγενεῖς}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1430; οἱ ἔξω γένους [prev. author] “Ant.” 660; οὐδὲν ἐν γένει [prev. author] “OT” 1016; γένει προσήκειν τινί 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.6.1; γένει ἀπωτέρω εἶναι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 44.13: in _genitive_, γένους εἶναί τινος}} to be of his **race**, ἄναγνος καὶ γένους τοῦ Λαΐου {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1383, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.2.9}}; ἐγγυτέρω, ἐγγύτατα γένους, nearer, next **of kin**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.33, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 388.}}
**2** **direct descent**, opposed to **collateral relationship**, γένος γάρ, ἀλλ᾽ οὐχὶ συγγένεια {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 8.33}}; αἱ κατὰ γένος βασιλεῖαι **hereditary** monarchies, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1285a16, 1313a10.}}
**II** **offspring**, even of **a single descendant**, σὸν γ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.124, 21.186; ἡ δ᾽ ἄρ᾽ ἔην θεῖον γ. οὐδ᾽ ἀνθρώπων 6.180; ἁμὸν Οἰδίπου γ. 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 654}}; Διὸς γ., of Bacchus, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1117 (Lyric poetry); Τέκμησσα, δύσμορον γ. [prev. author] “Aj.” 784.}}
**II.2** collectively, **offspring, posterity**, ἐκεῖνοι καὶ τὸ γ. τὸ ἀπ᾽ ἐκείνων {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.126; ἐξώλη ποιεῖν αὐτὸν καὶ γ. καὶ οἰκίαν 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.71.}}
**III** generally, **race**, of beings, θεῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Thesmophoriazusae” 960; ἡμιθέων γ. ἀνδρῶν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.23}}; ἡμιόνων, βοῶν γ., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.852, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 20.212}}; ἵππειον γ., i.e. **mules**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 342; ἰχθύων πλωτὸν γ. [prev. author] “fragment” 941.9.}}
**III.b** **clan, house, family**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.125, etc.; Φρὺξ μὲν γενεῇ, γένεος δὲ τοῦ βασιληΐου [prev. work] 35}}; τοὺς ἀπὸ γένους men **of noble family**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Romulus” 21; ἱερεὺς κατὰ γ. “IG” 5(1).497, +others; also ἱέρεια ἀπὸ γένους, διὰ γένους, [prev. work]607.29,602; especially at Athens and elsewhere as a subdivision of the φρατρία, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ath. fragment” 3, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.120e, etc.}}; = Latin _gens_, {{Refs 1st c.BC+|1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 4.21, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Numa” 1.}}
**III.c** **tribe**, as a subdivision of ἔθνος, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.56, 101.}}
**III.d** **caste**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.164.}}
**III.e** of animals, **breed**, {{Refs|[prev. author] 4.29.}}
**III.2** **age, generation**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.245}}; γ. χρύσεον, etc., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 109}}: hence, **age, time of life**, γένει ὕστερος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.215, compare 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1408a27.}}
**IV** **sex**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 172.1, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Symposium” 189d}}; **gender**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica” 1407b7, 2nd c.BC: Diogenes Babylonius Stoicus 3.214, etc.}}
**V** **class, sort, kind**, τὰ γ. τῶν κυνῶν ἐστι δισσά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Cynegeticus” 3.1; τὸ φιλόσοφον γ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 501e}}; τὸ τῶν γεωργῶν [γ.] {{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Ti.” 17c, compare “R.” 434b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1329a27; τῶν ἰχθυοπωλῶν γ. 4th c.BC: Xenarchus Comicus 7.4; τὸ τῶν παρασίτων γ. Nicolaus Rhetor Comedy texts 1.1, etc.}}
**V.2** in Logic, opposed to εἶδος (species), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 129c, +others, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Topica” 102a31, 102b12, +others; τὰ γ. εἰς εἴδη πλείω καὶ διαφέροντα διαιρεῖται [prev. author] “Metaphorical” 1059b36.}}
**V.3** in the animal kingdom, τὰ μέγιστα γ., = the modern {{Refs|“Classes”}}, such as birds, fishes, {{Refs|[prev. author] “HA” 490b7, compare 505b26}}; so in the vegetable kingdom, γένη τὰ μέγιστα, ={σιτώδη, χεδροπά} and ἀνώνυμα, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 8.1.1.}}
**V.3.b** **genus**, τὸ τῶν καρκίνων γ., τὸ τῶν περιστερῶν γ., etc., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 487b17, 488a4; τῶν δένδρων καὶ τῶν φυτῶν εἴδη πλείω τυγχάνει καθ᾽ ἕκαστον γένος 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 1.14.3; τοῦ αὐτοῦ γένους [πίτυς] καὶ πεύκη 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 1.69, +others}}
**V.3.c** γένος τι a **species** of plant, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 4.8.13}}; so later, γένη, = **crops**, ἄλλοις γένεσι τοῖς πρὸς πυρὸν διοικουμένοις {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“PTeb.” 66.43, +others (2nd c.BC)}}; οἷς ἐὰν αἱρῶμαι γένεσι πλὴν κνήκου {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PAmh.” 2.91.15 (2nd c.AD)}}; **produce,** {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|POxy. 727.20 (2nd c.AD)}}; **materials**, {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|[prev. work]54.16 (3rd c.AD)}}; ἐν γένεσιν in **kind**, opposed to ἐν ἀργυρίῳ, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“PFay.” 21.10 (2nd c.AD).}}
**V.4** τὰ γ. the **elements**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Timaeus” 54b}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _jánas_, _genitive_ **jánasas**; Latin _genus_, **-eris**, see at {γίγνομαι}.)

1087:
** γερουσί-α**, ἡ,
**Council of Elders, senate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 401: especially at Sparta, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 20.107, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1270b24, “IG” 5(2).345.10 (Orchom. 4th c.AD(?): Arcadius Grammaticus, 2nd-1st c.BC)}}; compare γερωΐα and γεροντί; also of the Carthaginian {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“Senate”, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1272b37}}; and the Roman, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.789e, 4th c.AD: Julianus Imperator “Orationes” 2.97b}}; of the Jewish **Sanhedrin,** {{LXX+NT|NT.Acts.5.21, cf. LXX.Exo.3.16.}}
**2** **sacred college**, ἱερὰ γ. {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|“IG” 3.702 (from Eleusis), compare 7.2808 (Hyettus, 3rd c.BC), etc.}}
**II** ={πρεσβεία}, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Rhesus” 936.}}

1088:
** γέρων**, οντος, ὁ,
**old man**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.33, etc.: pleonastic, παλαιοὶ γέροντες 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 676; ἄνους τε καὶ γ. 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 281, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1349; ἀεὶ γὰρ ἡβᾷ τοῖς γέρουσιν εὖ μαθεῖν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 584; καλὸν δὲ καὶ γέροντα μανθάνειν σοφά [prev. author] “fragment” 396.}}
**2** γέροντες, οἱ, **Elders, Chiefs**, κίκλησκεν δὲ γέροντας ἀριστῆας Παναχαιῶν [{{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.404 {γερωνία}, compare 9.574, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.14; later, “Senators”, especially at Sparta, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.65, 6.57, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 692a, “IG” 22.687, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1265b38 (_sing._ γέροντι “IG” 5(1).1346, but usually γερουσίας, which see)}}]; in other states, as at Elis, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1306a17, compare 1272a7, “OGI” 479.11 (from Dorylaeum).}}
**II** as _adjective_, **old**, γέρον σάκος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 22.184; γ. γράμμα 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 331; more frequently in _masculine_, γ. πατήρ 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.358, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 18.53; ἀνὴρ γ. 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1351; γ. χαλκός 4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 144; γ. λόγος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Agamemnon” 750 (Lyric poetry); ἵππος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 25; πόνος [prev. author] “OC” 1258 (codices but πίνος Scaliger, editions)}}; οἶνος {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 167.5, compare 4th c.BC: Eubulus Comicus 124}}; πέπλος, λέμβος, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 7.17, 21.12}}: rarely in Prose, οἱ γέροντες τῶν ἰχθύων {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 607b28}}; of stags, {{Refs|[prev. work] 611b3}}; Ἀντίγονος ὁ γ. Antigonus **the Elder**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Pelopidas” 2: _neuter_ _plural_, γέροντα βουλεύεις (for ἀρχαῖα) 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 794.}}
**III** **part of the spinning-wheel**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pherecrates Comicus 114}}. (Sanskrit _járant-_ 'old', **járati** 'render infirm'; compare γέρας.)

1089:
** γεύω**,
_future_ γεύσω {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Anaxippus Comicus 1.27}}: _aorist_ ἔγευσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.46, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 149}}: —_middle_, _future_ γεύσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.413, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἐγευσάμην {{Refs|20.181, etc.}}; γεύσεται, -σόμεθα, _Epic dialect_ for -ηται, -ώμεθα, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.61, 20.258}}: _3rd.pers._ _plural_ _optative_ γευσαίατο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 423}}: _perfect_ γέγευμαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus see below, etc. (γεύμεθα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.51)}}: _pluperfect_ ἐγέγευντο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.70}}:—**give a taste** of, γλυκὺν γεύσας τὸν αἰῶνα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.46; τινά τι 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Cyclops” 149, 5th c.BC: Theopompus Comicus 65, 2nd c.AD: Polyaenus Historicus 1.1.1; τινά τινος 4th c.BC: Anaxippus Comicus [same place], 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 179, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 634a: _metaphorically_, τινὰ ἀγαθῶν λόγων uncertain in 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Γεωργός” 45; σ᾽ ἔγευσ᾽ ἂν τῶν ἐμῶν χειρῶν 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 6.11: but}}
**II** _middle_, γεύομαι, with _perfect_ and _pluperfect_ _passive_, **taste**, with _genitive_, προικὸς γεύσεσθαι Ἀχαιῶν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 17.413}}; ἀλλήλων ἐγέγευντο **they had tasted, eaten of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.70; μέλιτος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 559d, etc.}}
**II.2** **take food**, {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 3.1.β, NT.Act.10.10}}; **dine, PLond. unpublished**{{Refs 4th c.AD+|2487 (4th c.AD).}}
**II.3** _metaphorically_, **taste, make proof of, feel**, δουρὸς ἀκωκῆς ἡμετέροιο γεύσεται {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.60; ὀϊστοῦ 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.98; χειρῶν 20. 181}}; ἀλλ᾽ ἄγε.. γευσόμεθ᾽ ἀλλήλων ἐγχείῃσιν **let us try** one another with the spear, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 20.258}}; **taste the sweets of**, ἀρχῆς, ἐλευθερίης, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.147, 6.5; ὕμνων 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “I.” 5(4).20}}; ἀλκᾶς, στεφάνων, {{Refs|[prev. author] “P.” 9.35, “I.” 1.21; εἰ δὲ γεύεται ἀνδρὸς ἀνήρ τι [prev. author] “N.” 7.86}}; of a married woman, ἀνδρὸς γεγευμένη {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 243}}; γ. πόνων **to have experience of** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 6.24; μόχθων 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Trachiniae” 1101; πένθους 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Alcestis” 1069; νόμων 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 752c; ἀμφοτέρων [prev. author] “R.” 358e}}; γ. ἐμπύρων **make trial of** them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1005: rarely with _accusative_, ἔρσης ἰκμάδα γευόμενος “Anthologia Graeca” 6.120 (1st c.AD: Leonidas Alexandrinus Epigrammaticus); κάππαριν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.687d: _absolutely_, 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 844}}. (Cf. Sanskrit _ju[snull]áte_ 'enjoy', Latin _gusto._)

1090:
** γεωργ-έω**,
**to be a husbandman, farmer**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 805e, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 14.2, etc.; γ. ἐν τῇ γῇ 5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.92; ἐν τῇ Νάξῳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Euthyphro” 4c, etc.}}; γεωργεῖς ἐκ τούτων you **have become a landed proprietor** by these means (i. e. the fruits of treason), {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.314: with _accusative_ _cognate_, γεωργίαν ζῶσαν γ.}} of pastoral nomads, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1256a35:}} —_middle_, οἱ γεωργούμενοι {{Refs|Aristeas 112.}}
**II** with _accusative_, **till, plough, cultivate**, πολλήν (i.e. γῆν) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ecclesiazusae” 592}}; τὰς ἄλλας [νήσους] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.88; γεωργῶν τὰ ἐκείνων 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.41}}:—_passive_, of land, {{Refs|“IG” 9(1).61 (from Daulis)}}; χώρας γεγεωργημένης καὶ γεωργηθησομένης {{Refs|“SIG” 685.80 (from Crete)}}; τὰ γεωργούμενα φυτά {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 896a10.}}
**II.2** generally, **cultivate**, ἐλαίαν {{Refs|“Geoponica” 9.2.6: hence, γ. ἔλαιον, οἶνον}}, **produce** it, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 49.36, compare “IG” 22.1100; τοῦ γεωργουμένου οἴνου “Geoponica” 6.7.2.}}
**II.3** _metaphorically_, **work at** a thing, **practise** or **exploit** it, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 25.82; φιλίαν 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.776b; τέχνην 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 6.6; τὸν ἱππόδρομον 4th c.AD: Libanius Sophista “Orationes” 35.13}}; **cultivate**, ψυχὰς δόγμασι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st c.AD: Philo Judaeus 2.348.}}
**II.4** of a river, **fertilize**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Philostratus Sophista “Philostrati majoris imagines” 1.11, “Ep.” 59, 3rd c.AD: Heliodorus Scriptor Eroticus 2.28.}}

1091:
** γεώργ-ιον**, τό,
**field**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd-2nd c.BC: Philo Mechanicus “(Belopoeica) Excerpte aus Philons Mechanik - Ath. Berl. Akad.” 96.49 (_plural_), Theagenes Historicus 17 (_plural_), “BGU” 1092.10 (4th c.AD)}}; **orchard**, {{NT+1st c.BC+|1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 14.5.6: _metaphorically_, Θεοῦ γ. NT.1Cor.3.9.}}
**II** **husbandry**, {{LXX|LXX.Sir.27.6.}}
**III** **crop**,{{LXX|LXX.Pro.24.5.}}
**IV** in _plural_, **tax** on land, uncertain in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|“SIG” 311.9 (Lagina, 4th c.BC).}}

1093:
** γῆ**, ἡ,
occasionally in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, frequently in 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus, and the only form in _Attic dialect_ Prose for γαῖα}}: dual γαῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Persae” 736: _plural_ rare, γαῖ 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 934b9, γέαι “SIG” 279.40 (from Zelea), etc., “Anthologia Graeca” 9.430 (1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Crinagoras Epigrammaticus): _genitive_ γεῶν 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.198, “GDI” 5755.14 (from Mylasa)}}; γῶν {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“BGU” 993 iii 10 (2nd c.BC): _accusative_ γέας [3rd c.AD: Democritus Epigrammaticus] 299, “SIG” 46.3 (Halic.), γᾶς “PTeb.” 6.31 (2nd c.BC), 1st c.BC-1st c.AD: Strabo Geographus 2.5.26; 5th c.AD: Cypria ζᾶς “Inscription Cyprian dialect” 135.30 H.: _dative_ _plural_ γέαις probably in “CIG” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” “Corpus Inscriptionum Graecarum” 2693f9 (from Mylasa), “LW” 415.9 ([prev. passage])}}:—**earth** (including land and sea, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica “Papyrus fragments” in E. Diehl, 5.2)}} opposed to **heaven**, or **land** opposed to **sea**, Γῆ τε καὶ Ἠέλιος καὶ Ἐρινύες {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.259, compare 3.104}}; τίς γ; {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.233; γῆς περίοδοι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.36, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 362b12, title of work by 4th-5th c.BC: Hecataeus Milesius Historicus: personified, 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” [same place], 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 69, “Pers.” 629, etc.}}; κατὰ γῆν on **land**, by **land**, opposed to ναυσί, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.18}}; opposed to ἐκ θαλάσσης, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] 2.81; κατὰ γῆν στέλλεσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.5, etc.}}; ἐπὶ γῆς on **earth**, opposed to νέρθε, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 416}}; κατὰ γῆς below **the earth**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 377, 475, etc.; κάτω γῆς 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 968; ὑπὸ γῆς [prev. author] “fragment” 572; γᾶς ὑπένερθε 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “Fragmenta.” 292}}: _genitive_ with local Adverbs, ἵνα γῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 168}}; ποῦ, ποῖ, ὅποι γ., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 108, “Ph.” 1211, “El.” 922; ὅπου γ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Aves” 9.}}
**2** **earth**, as an element, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 27, 5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus 4, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 320d, “Lg.” 889b, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Metaphysica” 989a5, “Cael.” 306a18, etc.}}
**2.b** γῆν καὶ ὕδωρ αἰτεῖν, as tokens of submission, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.18, 4th c.BC: Lycurgus Orator 71; γῆν καὶ ὕδωρ διδόναι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.18, +others}}
**II** **land, country**, καὶ γῆν καὶ πόλιν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 993}}; γῆν πρὸ γῆς ἐλαύνεσθαι, διώκειν, from **land to land**, {{LXX+5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Pr.” 682, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 235; ἡ ἁγία γῆ LXX.Wis.12.3}}; one's **native land**, {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th c.BC: Tyrtaeus Elegiacus 12.33, 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 1213, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 890 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 441, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Phoenissae” 1090}}; frequently omitted with _article_, ἐκ τῆς ἐμαυτοῦ (i.e. γῆς) δραπέτας {{Refs|[prev. author] “Heracl.” 140, etc.}}
**II.2** frequently in Trag., **city**, αὐτός τε καὶ γῆ δορὶ πεσοῦσ᾽ Ἑλληνικῷ {{Refs|[prev. author] “Tr.” 868.}}
**III** **the earth** or **ground** as tilled, ἄροτον γῆς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 270; γᾶ φθίνουσα [prev. work] 665}}, etc.; τὴν γῆν ἐργάζεσθαι, θεραπεύειν, till **the ground**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 420e, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 5.12; τὰ ἐκ τῆς γῆς φυόμενα [prev. author] “Mem.” 4.3.10.}}
**III.2** **estate, farm**, γῆν πρίασθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 32.23}}; ἐπὶ γῇ δανείζειν lend on mortgage, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 36.6.}}
**IV** of particular **kinds of earth** or **minerals**, e.g. **fuller's earth.** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Characteres” 10.14, compare 2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 12.168; Κιμωλία γ. 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 712, compare 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “γυναικεῖα” 2.189.}}

1094:
** γῆρας**, τό,
_genitive_ γήραος {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.60, +others, 7th c.BC: Archilochus Lyricus 116, 6th c.BC: Mimnermus Lyricus 2.6, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 8.71, etc.; γήρως 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 174, _Attic dialect_ (see. below): _dative_ γήραϊ 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 7.99, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.24}}, _contraction_ γήρᾳ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 507, etc., γήρατι variant in 4th c.AD: Adamantius Physiognomonicus “Phgn.” 1.14 (compare γῆρος)}}:—**old age**, γ. λυγρόν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 24.250; στυγερόν 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.336; ἐπὶ γήραος οὐδῷ (see. οὐδός) 22.60}}; opposed to γ. λιπαρόν, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.368, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus [same place]; γ. πολιόν 6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 174}}; γῆρας ἐκδῦναι, ἀποσείσασθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Pax” 336, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 670 (with play on signification 11)}}; ἐπὶ γήρως in **old age**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Eq.” 524; ἐν τῷ γήρᾳ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 329c, 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 2.73}}; σὺν γήρᾳ, ἐν γ. βαρύς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 17, “Aj.” 1017; διανοίας γ. 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Politica” 1271a1: _metaphorically_, οὐκ ἔστι γῆρας τοῦδε τοῦ μιάσματος}}, i.e. it never wears out, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 682.}}
**II** **cast skin, slough of a serpent**, γῆρας ἐκδύνειν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 549b26, 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 31, 3rd c.BC: Antigonus Carystius Paradoxographus “Mirabilia” 20, 2nd c.AD: Antyllus Medicus cited in 4th c.AD: Oribasius Medicus 10.35.4}}; of crabs, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Historia Animalium” 600b20, 4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 177.}}
** γῆρος**, τό,
={γῆρας}, not found in _nominative_, _genitive_ γήρους {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ τῶν ἐντὸς παθῶν” 6, “Rev.Arch.” 1913.247 (from Selymbria), _dative_ γήρει LXX.Gen.15.15, +others, NT.Luke.1.36}}; γήρεϊ variant cited in {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Scholia 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.150. (These forms are also found as variants for γήραος, γήραϊ.)}}

1095:
** γηράσκω**,
_future_ γηράσομαι [ᾱ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Critias Philosophus, Tragicus, Elegiacus 1.5 (and in compounds, ἐγ-, κατα-, συγ-, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.18, 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 1308, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Fragmenta” 1058)}}; γηράσω {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 393e}}: _poetry_ _infinitive_ γηρᾱσέμεν {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 85.9}}: _aorist_ **ἐγήρᾱσα** (κατ-) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.146, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 202d (also causal, compare below 11): _accusative_ _feminine_}}_participle_ γηράσασαν (variant{γηρᾶσαν}) {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.114}}: _perfect_ γεγήρᾱκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 727, etc.}}:—also **γηράω** {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 4.1.15, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Ethica Nicomachea” 1135b2, 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 481.14, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.911b}}, _participle_ γηρῶν {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Epictetus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 3:}}_aorist 2_ (as if from γήρημι or γήρᾱμι) ἐγήρα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 7.148, 17.197, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 14.67, (κατ-) 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 6.72}}; _infinitive_ γηράναι [ᾰ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 908 (cum Scholia), 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 870 (so “Etymologicum Magnum” 230.53, but γηρᾶναι 2nd c.AD: Moeris Grammaticus 115)}}, _participle_ γηράς {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.197, _dative_ _plural_ γηράντεσσι 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 188}}, _genitive_ _plural_ [ὑπερ-] γηράντων uncertain in {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 7.17}}; also γηρείς, έντος, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 9}}:—_middle_, γηράσκομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 171}}:—_passive_, (ὑπερ-) γηραθείς {{Refs 3rd c.AD+|3rd c.AD: Pseudo-Callisthenes Historicus 1.25}}:—**grow old**, and in _aorist_ and _perfect_, **to be so**, κηρύσσων γήρασκε **grew old** in his office of herald, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 17.325, compare 2.663, etc.}}; of things, ὄγχνη ἐπ᾽ ὄγχνῃ γ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 7.120; χρόνος γηράσκων 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 981; πάλιν γὰρ αὖθις παῖς ὁ γ. ἀνήρ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Fragmenta” 487; μετὰ τὴν δόσιν γ. χάρις 4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus “Μονόστιχοι - Fragmenta Comicorum Graecorum” 347; τὸ τῆσδε χώρας οὐ γεγήρακε σθένος 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 727: with _accusative_ _cognate_, βίον τοιοῦτον γηράναι [prev. work] 870}}:—so in _middle_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Fragmenta” 171.}}
**II** causal in _aorist 1_ ἐγήρᾱσα, **bring to old age**, ἐγήρασάν με τροφῇ {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 894}}; γηράσας πόδα (but perhaps _accusative_ _cognate_) {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“Anthologia Graeca” 6.94 (1st c.AD: Philippus Epigrammaticus). (Akin to γέρων, γῆρας.)}}

1096:
** γίγνομαι**,
_Ionic dialect_ and after {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus}} **γίνομαι** [ῑ], (_Attic dialect_ Inscrr. have γιγν- in fifth and fourth cententury,{{Refs|compare “IG” 2.11.9, 1055.25, etc.)}}; Thess. **γίνυμαι** {{Refs|“IG” 9(2).517.22}}; _Boeotian dialect_ **γίνιουμαι** {{Refs|[prev. work]7.3303:}}_future_ γενήσομαι: _aorist_ ἐγενόμην (ἐγενάμην {{LXX|LXX.Jer.14.1, +others}} (προ-) Decrees Byzantine cited in {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.90)}}, _Ionic dialect_ _2nd pers._ _singular_ γένευ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.897}}, _3rd.pers._ _singular_ γενέσκετο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 11.208, ἔγεντο 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 705, 7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 16, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 3.87, 4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.20, “IG” 4.492 (from Mycenae), probably in “Scol.” 19}}; _Epic dialect_ γέντο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Theogonia” 199, 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 98.5, 3rd c.BC: Callimachus Epicus “Hymnus in Jovem” 1.50, 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 14.27, etc.}} (gṇ-το): _perfect_ γέγονα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 19.122, etc.}}: _3rd.pers._ _plural_ γέγοναν {{NT|NT.Rev.21.6}}: _pluperfect_ ἐγεγόνει {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 31.17, etc.}}; _Ionic dialect_ ἐγεγόνεε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.2}}; _Epic dialect_ forms (as if from _perfect_ γέγᾰα), _2nd pers._ _plural_ γεγάᾱτε {{Refs 8th c.BC+|Batrachomyomachia 143; γεγάᾱσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.325, frequently in 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey”: _3rd.pers._ _plural_ γεγᾱκᾰσιν conjecture in 5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 23.10: _3rd.pers._ _dual_}}_pluperfect_ ἐκ-γεγάτην [ᾰ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 10.138}}; _infinitive_ γεγάμεν [ᾰ] {{Refs 8th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 9.110, (ἐκ) 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.248, etc.}}; _participle_ γεγᾰώς -ᾰυῖα, _plural_ -ᾰῶτες, -ᾰυῖαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus, etc.}}, _contraction_ γεγώς, -ῶσα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 472, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 406}}; _infinitive_ γεγᾱκειν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.49}}: _middle_ forms ἐκγεγάασθε epigram {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus 16, ἐκ-γεγάονται (in _future_ sense) “hymnus ad Venerem” 197 (assuming variant)}}:—_passive_ forms, _future_ γενηθήσομαι (only in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Parmenides” 141e, οὔτε γενήσεται, οὔτε γενηθήσεται,compare 5th c.AD: Proclus Philosophus “in Platonis Parmenidem commentarii” p.963 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus)}}: _aorist_ ἐγενήθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 209, 4th c.BC: Archytas Tarentinus Philosophus 1, 5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “ἐπιδημίαι” 6.8.32, 7.3}}, later _Attic dialect_, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philemo Comicus 95.2 and 167, “IG” 2.630b10 (1st c.BC)}} and Hellenistic Gk., {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 2.67.8, 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 13.51}}: _perfect_ γεγένημαι {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Simonides Lyricus 69}}, frequently in _Attic dialect_ Poets and Prose, in _Attic dialect_ _inscription_ first in 4th C, {{Refs|“IG” 2.555}}: _3rd.pers._ _plural_ γεγενέανται {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Philetas cited in 12th c.AD: Eustathius Episcopus Thessalonicensis 1885.51}}: _pluperfect_ ἐγεγένητο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.18, +others}}; compare γείνομαι:—**come into a new state of being**: hence,
**I** _absolutely_, **come into being** opposed to εἶναι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 17.11, 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 102e, compare “Ti.” 29a}}; and so,
**I.1** of persons, **to be born**, νέον γεγαώς new **born**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 19.400}}; ὑπὸ Τμώλῳ γεγαῶτας **born** (and so **living**) under Tmolus, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.866; ἢ πρόσθε θανεῖν ἢ ἔπειτα γ. 8th-7th c.BC: Hesiodus Epicus “Opera et Dies” 175}}; γιγνομέναισι λάχη τάδ᾽.. ἐκράνθη **at our birth**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Eumenides” 347; γ. ἔκ τινος 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.548, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.11; πατρὸς ἐκ ταὐτοῦ 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Iphigenia Aulidensis” 406, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 5.136; σέθεν.. ἐξ αἵματος 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 142; less frequently ἀπό τινος 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 8.22, etc.; ἐσθλῶν 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 380, etc.}}; γεγονέναι κακῶς, καλῶς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 218, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 7.37, etc.}}; κάλλιον, εὖ, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.146, 3.69}}; τὸ μὴ γενέσθαι not **to have been born**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Fragmenta - American Journal of Philology” 401: frequently with Numerals, ἔτεα τρία καὶ δέκα γεγονώς 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.119; ἀμφὶ τὰ πέντε ἢ ἑκκαίδεκα ἔτη γενόμενος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.4.16; γεγονὼς ἔτη περὶ πεντήκοντα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.154}}; οἱ ὑπὲρ τὰ στρατεύσιμα ἔτη γεγονότες **those of an age** beyond.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.4: with _genitive_, γεγονὼς πλειόνων ἐτῶν ἢ πεντήκοντα 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Leges” 951c, etc.}}: rarely with ordinals, ὀγδοηκοστὸν ἔτος γεγονώς {{Refs 1st c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Macr.” 22, compare 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Philopoemen” 18.}}
**I.2** of things, **to be produced**, ὅσα φύλλα καὶ ἄνθεα γίγνεται ὥρῃ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.51}}; opposed to ὄλλυσθαι, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Parmenides Poeta Philosophus 8.13, 40}}; opposed to ἀπόλλυσθαι, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Anaxagoras Philosophus 17, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 527b, etc.}}; opposed to ἀπολείπειν, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Diogenes Cynicus Philosophus}} Apoll.7; opposed to ἀπολήγειν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Empedocles Poeta Philosophus 17.30; τὰ γιγνόμενα καὶ ἐξ ὧν γίγνεται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 27a; ἁπλῇ διηγήσει ἢ διὰ μιμήσεως γ. [prev. author] “R.” 392d; ὁ ἐκ τῆς χώρας γιγνόμενος σῖτος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 3.6.13; τὰ ἐν ἀγρῷ γιγνόμενα [prev. work] 2.9.4}}; of profits, καρποὶ οἱ ἐξ ἀγελῶν γ. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 1.1.2, etc.}}; τὰ ἆθλα ἀπὸ τεττάρων ταλάντων ἐγένοντο **were the produce of**, i.e. **were worth**, {{Refs|4 talents, [prev. author] “HG” 4.2.7}}; τὸ ἀπὸ τῶν αἰχμαλώτων γενόμενον ἀργύριον **produced by** [the ransom of].., {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 5.3.4}}; of sums, ὁ γεγονὼς ἀριθμὸς τῶν ψήφων the **total** of the votes, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 36a; ἕκατον εἴκοσι στατήρων γίγνονται τρισχίλιαι τριακόσιαι ἑξήκοντα [δραχμαί] 120}} staters **amount to** {{Refs 4th c.BC+|3, 360 drachmae, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 34.24}}; so in in Mathematics texts, of **products**, ὁ ἐξ αὐτῶν γενόμενος ἀριθμός {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 7.24}}; ἀριθμὸς γενόμενος ἑκατοντάκις **multiplied**{{Refs 3rd c.AD+|by 100, 3rd c.AD(?): Pappus Mathematicus 10.13}}; of times of day, ὡς ἡ ἡμέρα ἐγένετο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.81, etc.; ἕως ἂν φῶς γένηται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 311a; ἅμα ἕῳ γιγνομένῃ 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.32}}; of Time in general, **elapse**, διέτης χρόνος ἐγεγόνεε ταῦτα πρήσσουσι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.2; χρόνου γενομένου 1st c.BC: Diodorus Siculus Historicus 20.109.}}
**I.2.b** **falldue**, οἱ γιγνόμενοι δασμοί {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.1.8; τοὺς τόκους τοὺς γ. 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 17.37}}; τὸ τίμημα τὸ γ., τὸ γ. ἀργύριον, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 24.82, Syngr. cited in [same work] 35.11; τὸ γ. μέρος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.4.33; τὸ γ. τοῖ πλήθι τᾶς ζαμίαυ “IG” 5(2).6 A 20 (Tegea, 4th c.BC)}}: with _dative_, τὸ γ. τινὶ ἔλαιον {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|“UPZ” 19.32 (2nd c.BC)}}; τοῖς γείτοσι τὸ γ. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Fragmenta” 97; τὰ γ.}}**dues,** {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|PHib. 1.92 and 111 (3rd c.BC): hence γιγνόμενος}} **regular, normal**, τίμημα, χάρις, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 38.25}}; ἐν ταῖς γ. ἡμέραις in the **usual number** of days, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 5.4.51}}; frequently in later Gk., as {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Lucianus Sophista “Tox.” 18, etc.}}
**I.3** of events, **take place, come to pass**, and in past tenses **to be**, καί σφιν ἄχος κατὰ θυμὸν ἐγίγνετο {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.86, etc.; μάχη ἐγεγόνει 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Charmides” 153b, etc.; ἐκεχειρία γίγνεταί τισι πρὸς ἀλλήλους 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.58; ἡ νόσος ἤρξατο γίγνεσθαι [prev. author] 2.47; πνεῦμα εἰώθει γ. [prev. work] 84}}; τὰ Ὀλύμπια γίγνεται, τραγῳδοὶ γίγνονται, **are held**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 7.4.28, 4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 3.41, etc.}}; ψήφισμα γ. **is passed**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.2.21}}; πιστὰ γ., ὅρκοι γ., pledges **are given**, oaths **taken**,{{Refs 4th c.BC+|[prev. work] 7.4.3, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 19.158}}; γίγνεταί τι ὑπό τινος (_masculine_), {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.1.30, (_neuter_) 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 200e; τὰ γιγνόμενα ὑπὸ τῶν Ἀθηναίων 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.88; τὰ γενόμενα ἐξ ἀνθρώπων 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus “Praef.”; ὕβρισμα ἐκ τῶν Σαμίων γενόμενον [prev. author] 3.48; ἀπό τινος γ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 5.6.30; παρά τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 614a}}; ὃ μὴ γένοιτο which **God forbid**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.27, 28.21}}; but γένοιτο, = {{LXX|“Amen”, LXX.Isa.25.1}}; γένοιτο γένοιτο{{LXX|LXX.Psa.71(72).19: in Mathematics texts, γεγονέτω}} **suppose it done**, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Euclides Geometra 6.23, etc.; γέγονε}}**it is done,** {{NT|NT.Rev. 16.17: with _dative_ et}}_participle_, γίγνεταί τί μοι βουλομένῳ, ἀσμένῳ (see. βούλομαι, ἄσμενος); οὐκ ἂν ἐμοί γε ἐλπομένῳ τὰ γένοιτο, i.e. I could not hope to see these things **take place**, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 3.228; ἡδομένοισι ἡμῖν οἱ λόγοι γεγόνασι 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.46}}, etc.; of sacrifices, omens, etc., οὐ γάρ σφι ἐγίνετο τὰ σφάγια χρηστά{{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. work] 61, compare 62; τὰ ἱερὰ καλὰ ἐγένετο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.4.9: _absolutely_, τὰ διαβατήρια ἐγ}}. **were favourable**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.55; θυομένῳ οὐκ ἐγίγνετο τὰ ἱερά 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 3.1.17: in _neuter_}}_participle_, τὸ γενόμενον **the event, the fact**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 6.54}}; τὰ γενόμενα **the facts**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.1.9, etc.; τὸ γιγνόμενον 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Theaetetus” 161b, etc.}}; τὰ γεγενημένα **the past**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 6.2.14}}; τὸ γενησόμενον **the future**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.138}}; τὰ γεγονότα, opposed to ὄντα, μέλλοντα, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 392d, compare “Lg.” 896a: of Time, ὡς τρίτη ἡμέρη τῷ παιδίῳ ἐκκειμένῳ ἐγένετο 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.113; ἕως ἄν τινες χρόνοι γένωνται 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 108c}}; but in _perfect_ and _pluperfect_, **to have passed**, ὡς διετὴς χρόνος ἐγεγόνεε {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.2; πρὶν ἓξ μῆνας γεγονέναι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Protagoras” 320a: _impersonal_, ἐγένετο or γέγονεν ὥστε}}.. **it happened, came to pass** that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 5.3.10, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.40, etc.}}; ἐγένετο, ὡς ἤκουσεν.. καὶ ἐθυμώθη **it came to pass**, when he heard.. that.., {{LXX+NT|LXX.Gen.39.19; ἐγένετο ἐν τῷ πορεύεσθαι.. καὶ διήρχετο NT.Luke.17.11: with}}_infinitive_, γίνεται εὑρεῖν **it is possible** to find, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 639}}; ἐγένετο, with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **it came to pass** that, {{NT+2nd c.AD+|NT.Act.9.3, +others, “PAmh.” 2.135.10 (2nd c.AD)}}: with _dative_ et _infinitive_, ἐάν σοι γένηται στραφῆναι {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Epictetus Philosophus “Enchiridion” 23.}}
**II** followed by a Predicate, **come into** a certain state, **become**, and (in past tenses), **to be**,
**II.1** followed by Nouns and _adjectives_, δηΐοισι δὲ χάρμα γ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 6.82, compare 8.282; σωτὴρ γενοῦ μοι 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Choephori” 2; κωλυτὴς γ. τινός 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.23; [οὖροι] νηῶν πομπῆες γ. 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.362, etc.}}; πάντα δὲ γιγνόμενος πειρήσεται **turning** every way, {{Refs|[prev. work] 417}}; παντοῖος γ., followed by μή, with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 3.124; παντοῖος γ. δεόμενος [prev. author] 7.10. ; ἐκ πλουσίου πένης γ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 7.7.28; δημοτικὸς ἐξ ὀλιγαρχικοῦ γ. 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 572d: rarely with}}_participle_, μὴ προδοὺς ἡμᾶς γένῃ, i.e. προδότης ἡμῶν, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 588, compare “Ph.” 773; μὴ ἀπαρνηθεὶς γένῃ 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Sophista” 217c; ἀποτετραμμένοι ἐγένοντο 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.68, etc.: with _pronoun_, τί γένωμαι}} **;** what **am I to become**, i.e. what is to become of me? {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Septem contra Thebas” 297, compare 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 15.51; οὐκ ἔχοντες ὅτι γένωνται 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.52; less frequently with _masculine_, οὐδ᾽ ἔχω τίς ἂν γενοίμαν 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 905; γίγνονται πάνθ᾽ ὅτι βούλονται 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 348.}}
**II.1.b** in past tenses, **having ceased to be**, ὁ γενόμενος στρατηγός the **ex**-strategus, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“POxy.” 38.11 (1st c.AD)}}; ἡ γ. γυνή τινος the **former** wife, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|“PFlor.” 99.4 (1st-2nd c.AD).}}
**II.2** with _adverbs_, κακῶς χρῆν Κανδαύλῃ γενέσθαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.8}}; εὖ, καλῶς, ἡδέως γ., **it goes** well, etc., {{LXX+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.7.5, 2nd c.AD: Arrianus Historicus “Epicteti Dissertationes” 3.24.97, LXX.Tob.7.9}}; with personal construction, οἱ παρὰ Πλάτωνι δειπνήσαντες ἐς αὔριον ἡδέως γίγνονται {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.127b}}; δίχα γ. τοῦ σώματος **to be parted from**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.20}}; τριχῇ γ. **to be** in three divisions, {{Refs|[prev. author] “An.” 6.2.16}}; γ. ἐμποδών, ἐκποδών, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hecuba” 372, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 6.5.38, etc.}}
**II.3** followed by oblique cases of Nouns,
**II.3.a** with _genitive_, γ. τῶν δικαστέων, τῶν γεραιτέρων, **become one of**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.25, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 1.2.15, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 107, etc.; βουλῆς γεγονώς 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 36.28 (compare above 1.b)}}; **fall to, belong to**, ἡ νίκη Ἀγησιλάου ἐγεγένητο {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.3.20}}; **to be under control of**, ὁ νοῦς ὅταν αὑτοῦ γένηται {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 660, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedrus” 250a (assuming variant); ὑμῶν αὐτῶν γενέσθαι 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.7 (also ἐντὸς ἑωυτοῦ γ. 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.119; ἐν ἑαυτῷ γ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.5.17; ἐν σαυτοῦ γενοῦ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Philoctetes” 950); τὴν πόλιν ἐλπίδος μεγάλης γινομένην 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Phocion” 23}}: of things, **to be at**, i.e. **cost**, so much, αἱ τριχίδες εἰ γενοίαθ᾽ ἑκατὸν τοὐβολοῦ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 662, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Oeconomicus” 20.23.}}
**II.3.b** with _dative_, **fall to**, i. e. as wife, {{LXX|LXX.Num.36.11.}}
**II.3.c** with Preps., γ. ἀπὸ δείπνου, ἐκ θυσίας, **have done**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 2.78, 1.50}}; πολὺν χρόνον γ. ἀπό τινος **to be separated** from.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 1.2.25}}; γ. εἴς τι **turn** into, τὸ κακὸν γ. εἰς ἀγαθόν {{LXX+6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Theognis Elegiacus 162; frequently in LXX, ἐγενήθη μοι εἰς γυναῖκα LXX.Gen.20.12}}; εἰς βρῶσιν{{LXX|LXX.Lam.4.10}}; εἰς οὐδέν, εἰς κενόν, {{NT+8th c.BC+|NT.Act.5.36, NT.1Thes.3.5; ἐς Αακεδαίμονα 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.38 (in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}} even without _preposition_, ἐμὲ χρεὼ γ. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 4.634)}}; γ. τι εἴς τινα **comes** to him, of a dowry, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Isaeus Orator 3.36}}; of a ward, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.117}}; γ. ἐξ ὀφθαλμῶν τινι **to be** out of sight, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.24}}; ἐξ ἀνθρώπων γ. **disappear** from.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Pausanias Periegeta 4.26.6; γ. ἐν Χίῳ 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 5.33, etc.}}; γ. ἐν.., **to be engaged** in.., οἱ ἐν ποιήσει γινόμενοι in poetry, {{Refs|[prev. author] 2.82}}; ἐν [πολέμῳ] {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.78; ἐν πείρᾳ γ. τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.9.1}}; ἐν ὀργῇ, ἐν αἰτίᾳ πρός τινα γ., {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Flamininus” 16, “Rom.” 7}}; of things, ἐν καιρῷ γ. **to be** in season, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 4.3.2; ἐν τύχῃ γ. τινί τι 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 4.73}}; γ. διὰ γηλόφων, of a road, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.4.24}}; but δι᾽ ἔχθρας γ. τινί **to be** at enmity with, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Ranae” 1412}}; γ. ἐπὶ ποταμῷ **arrive** or **be** at.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.189, etc.}}; γ. ἐπί τινι **fall into** or **be in** one's power, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 3.1.13, etc.; ἐπὶ συμφοραῖς γ. 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.58 codices (-ᾶς Schaefer)}}; γ. ἐπί τινι, also, **to be set** over.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 3.3.53}}; γ. ἐφ᾽ ἡμῶν αὐτῶν **to be** alone, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aeschines Orator 2. 36; γ. ἐπὶ τῆς διοικήσεως 2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 43.48}}; γ. ἐπ᾽ ἐλπίδος **to be** in hope, {{Refs 1st c.AD+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Solon” 14: in Mathematics texts, γ. ἐπὶ ἀριθμόν}} **to be multiplied** into a number, {{Refs|“Theologumena Arithmeticae” 3}}; γ. κατά τινα or τι **to be** near.. or **opposite to**.., in battle, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.14, “HG” 4.2.18}}; but κατὰ ξυστάσεις γ. **to be formed** into groups, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.21; καθ᾽ ἓν γ. [prev. author] 3.10}}; καθ᾽ αὑτοὺς γ. **to be** alone, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 10.52}}; γ. μετὰ τοῦ θείου **to be** with God, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 8.7.27, etc.; ἡ νίκη γ. σύν τινι [prev. author] “Ages.” 2.13}}; γ. παρ᾽ ἀμφοτέροις τοῖς πράγμασι **to be present** on both sides, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 5.26}}; γ. παρά τι **to depend** upon.., {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.232}}; γ. περὶ τὸ συμβουλεύειν **to be engaged** in.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 3.12}}; γενοῦ πρός τινα **go** to So-and-so, {{Refs|“PFay.” 128, etc.}}; γ. πρὸς τῇ καρδίᾳ **to be at** or **near**.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Phaedo” 118, etc.}}; γ. πρός τινι **to be engaged** in.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 12.270, 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.176}}; αὐτὸς πρὸς αὑτῷ **meditate**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.151c; so γ. πρὸς τὸ ἰᾶσθαι 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 604d; πρὸς παρασκευήν 2nd c.BC: Polybius Historicus 1.22.2: _impersonal_, ἐπεὶ πρὸς ἡμέραν ἐγίγνετο 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.4.6}}; γενέσθαι πρός τινων **to be** inclined towards them, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.22}}; γ. πρὸ ὁδοῦ **to be** forward on the way, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.382}}; γ. ὑπό τινι **to be** subject to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.11, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 7.64}}; γ. ὑπὸ ταῖς μηχαναῖς **to be** under the protection of.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.1.34.}}
**II.4** γίγνεται followed by _plural_ nouns, ἵνα γίγνηται.. ἀρχαί τε καὶ γάμοι {{NT+5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 363a, compare “Smp.” 188b; ἐγένετο.. ἡμέραι ὀκτώ NT.Luke.9.28}}. (Cf. **jánati** 'procreate', **jánas** (={γένος}), Latin _gigno_, **gnatus.**)

1097:
Included with: ** γιγνώσκω**,
_Doric dialect_ {{Refs 5th c.BC+|(5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 9, 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 6.97)}}, _Aeolic dialect_, _Ionic dialect_, and after {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus}} **γινώσκω**, but γιγνώσκω in early _Attic dialect_ Inscrr., as {{Refs|“IG” 12.127.19}} (κατα-), etc.: _future_ γνώσομαι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.497, etc.}}, _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _singular_ γνωσεῖται {{Refs|Callias Epigrammaticus “Lav. Pall.” 123 (γνώσω}} is _falsa lectio_ in {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “Steril.” 215)}}; Cretan dialect form ἀνα-γνώοντι uncertain in [{{Refs|“GDI” 5075 (for _aorist_ 1, see at {ἀναγιγνώσκω})}}]: _perfect_ ἔγνωκα {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.287, etc.}}: _aorist 2_ ἔγνων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 13.72, etc.}}, _Epic dialect_ dual γνώτην {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.36}}, _Doric dialect_ _3rd.pers._ _plural_ ἔγνον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 4.120; _imperative_ γνῶθι 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus [264], etc.}}; _subjunctive_ γνῶ, γνῷς, γνῷ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.411, etc.}}, _Epic dialect_ also γνώω, γνώομεν {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 16.304, γνώωσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 23.610; _optative_ γνοίην 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 18.125, etc.; _plural_ γνοῖμεν 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus Alcaeus1.129a}}; _infinitive_ γνῶναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 13.312, etc.}}, _Epic dialect_ γνώμεναι {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.266}}; _participle_ γνούς {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Electra” 731, etc.}}:—_middle_, _aorist 1_ γνώσασθαι {{Refs 4th c.AD+|4th c.AD(?): Manetho Astrologus 2.51}}:—_passive_, _future_ γνωσθήσομαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 918, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.124, etc.}}: _aorist_ ἐγνώσθην {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 7 (Lyric poetry), 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Electra” 852, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 2.65}}: _perfect_ ἔγνωσμαι {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Hercules Furens” 1287, 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.38}}:— **come to know, perceive**, and in past tenses, **know**, with _accusative_, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 12.272, etc.}}; as distinct from οἶδα **know by reflection**, γιγνώσκω, = **know by observation**, γνόντες δὲ εἰδότας περιορᾶν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.69; ἐγὼ δ᾽ οἶδ᾽ ὅτι γιγνώσκετε τοῦτον ἅπαντες 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.276}}; χαλεπόν ἐστι τὸ γνῶναι εἰ οἶδεν ἢ μή it is hard to **perceive** whether one knows or not, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Anthologia Graeca” 0.76a26}}; **discern, distinguish, recognize**, ὄφρ᾽ εὖ γιγνώσκῃς ἠμὲν θεὸν ἠδὲ καὶ ἄνδρα {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.128; ἀσπίδι γιγνώσκων by his shield, [prev. work] 182}}; ironically, εὖ νύ τις αὐτὸν γνώσεται he will **learn** him to his cost,{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|18.270; νῦν ἔγνων τὸν Ἔρωτα 3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 3.15: sometimes with _genitive_, γνώτην ἀλλήλων}} **were aware of**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 21.36, compare 23.109.}}
**2** followed by _relative_ clauses, γιγνώσκω δ᾽ ὡς.. I **perceive** that..,{{Refs 8th c.BC+|21.209; ἔγνως ὡς θεός εἰμι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 22.10; ἔγνωκας ὡς οὐδὲν λέγεις 5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Nubes” 1095}}; γ. ὅτι.. {{Refs 4th c.BC+|1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 108, 4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 104, 379, etc.}}; ἵν᾽ εἰδῆτε ὑμεῖς καὶ γνῶτε ὅτι.. {{Refs 8th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 21.143; γνώμεναι εἴ μιν.. φοβέουσι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 21.266; γ. τί πέπονθε πάθος 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Philebus” 60d: with _accusative_ and _relative_ clause, Τυδείδην δ᾽ οὐκ ἂν γνοίης, ποτέροισι μετείη 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 5.85; γ. θεοὺς οἵτινές εἰσι 1st c.AD(?): Heraclitus 5; Σωκράτην γ. οἷος ἦν 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Memorabilia” 4.8.11}}; τοὺς Πέρσας γ. ὅτι.. {{Refs|[prev. author] “Cyr.” 2.1.11; also ἀλλοτρίας γῆς γ. ὅτι δύναται φέρειν [prev. author] “Oec.” 16.3: with}}_participle_, ἔγνων μιν.. οἰωνὸν ἐόντα **perceived** that he was.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 15.532; γνόντες οὐδεμίαν σφίσι τιμωρίαν οὖσαν 5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.25; ἔγνωκα.. ἠπατημένη 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 807}}; ἔγνων ἡττημένος I **felt that** I **was** beaten, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Equites” 658; χρυσῷ πάττων μ᾽ οὐ γιγνώσκεις [prev. author] “Nu.” 912, compare 5th c.BC: Antipho Orator 5.33, 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 7.2.17: with _genitive_, ὡς γνῶ χωομένοιο}} when **he was aware of**.., {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 4.357, compare 5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Apologia” 27a}}: with _infinitive_, ἵνα γνῷ τρέφειν τὴν γλῶσσαν ἡσυχεστέραν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Antigone” 1089}}: with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, **recognize** that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 1.43, etc.}}; **take** a thing **to mean** that.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.78}}: with _double_ _accusative_, **perceive** or **know** another to be.., οἵους γνώσεσθε τοὺς ἀνθρώπους {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Anabasis” 1.7.4: _absolutely_, ὁ γιγνώσκων}} **the perceiver**, opposed to τὰ γιγνωσκόμενα **the objects perceived**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Respublica” 508e}}; also ὁ γ. **one who knows, a prudent person**, {{Refs|[prev. work] 347d}}:—_passive_, εἰ γνωσθεῖεν ᾧ.. if **it were known of them** in what.., {{Refs|[prev. author] “Prt.” 342b.}}
**II** **form a judgement, think**, ταὐτά {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 9.2; τἀναντία τούτοις γ. 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Historia Graeca (Hellenica)” 2.3.38; οὕτω γ. [prev. author] “An.” 6.1.19; τὰ δίκαια γ. 5th c.BC: Lysias Orator 22.2}}; ἃ γιγνώσκω λέγειν (={τὴν γνώμην λ}.) {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 4.1; περὶ τῆς βοηθείας ταῦτα γιγνώσκω [prev. author] 1.19}}; τοῦτο γιγνώσκων, ὅτι.. {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 572, compare 648; ὡς ἐμοῦ ἀγωνιουμένου οὕτω γίγνωσκε 5th-6th c.BC: Xenophon Historicus “Institutio Cyri (Cyropaedia)” 2.3.15: _absolutely_, αὐτὸς γνώσῃ}} **see** thou to that, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Gorgias” 505c}}; especially in dialogue, ἔγνων **I understand**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Ajax” 36}}; ἔγνως **you are right**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|[prev. author] “Tr.” 1221, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Andromache” 883}}; ἔγνωκα; Latin _tenes_? {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Nausicrates Comicus 1.5}}; **judge, determine, decree that**.., with _accusative_ et _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 1.74, 6.85, 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 17.16}}: with _infinitive_, **determine** to.., {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Andocides Orator 1.107}}:— _passive_, **to be pronounced**, of a sentence or judgement, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Thucydides Historicus 3.36; παρανόμως γνωσθεῖσα δίαιτα 4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 33.33, compare 59.47; κρίσις ἐγνωσμένη ὑπό τινος 5th-6th c.BC: Isocrates Orator 6.30.}}
**II.2** _passive_, of persons, **to be judged guilty**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Supplices” 7; γνωσθέντα ζημιοῦσιν οἱ νόμοι 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Rhetorica ad Alexandrum” 1431b30; τεθνάτω ἐὰν γνωσθῇ, ἐὰν δὲ φυγὴ γνωσθῇ, φευγέτω “IG” 12.10.29.}}
**II.3** _perfect_ _passive_ with _active_ sense, ὡμολόγηκεν ὑμᾶς ὑπάρχειν ἐγνωσμένους are **determined**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Demosthenes Orator 18.228 (sed to be read ἡμᾶς).}}
**III** **know carnally**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Menander Comicus 558.5, 4th c.AD: Heraclides Historicus “περὶ πολιτειῶν - fragment” 64, LXX.Gen.4.1, +others, NT.Matt.1.25, 1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus “Galba” 9, etc.}}
**IV** γ. χάριν, ={εἰδέναι χάριν}, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd-3rd c.AD: Dio Cassius Historicus 39.9.}}
**B** causal, **make known, celebrate**, γνώσομαι τὰν ὀλβίαν Κόρινθον {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 13.3 _accusative_ to Scholia (at [prev. work]), see uncertain (Root γνω-}}, cf. Sanskrit _jānāmi, jñātas_, Latin _gnosco_, **gnotus**, etc.)

1098:
** γλεῦκος**, εος
(_Doric dialect_ _genitive_ γλεύκιος {{Refs|“GDI” 4993 (from Gortyn))}}, τό, **sweet new wine**, {{NT+4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 380b32, 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 184, 299, “PPetr.” 3p.149 (3rd c.BC), NT.Act.2.13, 1st c.AD: Dioscorides (Dioscurides) Medicus 5.6; οἴνου γλεύκους “PGrenf.” 2.24.12 (2nd c.BC), “PFlor.” 65.8 (6th c.AD).}}
**2** **grape-juice**, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 6.575.}}
**II** **sweetness**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 931a18.}}

1099:
** γλῠκύς**, εῖα
(-ῆα{{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 4.2), ύ (-ύν “IG” 14.1890)}}, **sweet to the taste** or **smell**, νέκταρ {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 1.598; οἶνος 5th c.BC: Epicharmus Comicus 124, etc.; γλυκὺ ὄζειν 4th c.BC: Cratinus Junior Comicus 1, probably in 5th c.BC: Crates Comicus 2}}; opposed to ὀξύς, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Hippocrates Medicus “περὶ διαίτης” 2.55}}; opposed to δριμύς, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|1st-2nd c.AD: Plutarchus Biographus et Philosophus 2.708e: mostly _metaphorically_, even in 8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus}}, **pleasant, delightful**, ἵμερος, ὕπνος, {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 3.139, 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 2.395; γ. αἰών 5.152, 5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 7.46; πόλεμος γλυκίων γένετ᾽ ἠὲ νέεσθαι 8th c.BC: Ilias Homerus Epicus “Illiad” 2.453; οὐδὲν γλύκιον ἧς πατρίδος οὐδὲ τοκήων 8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.34, compare 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “N.” 5.2, 5th c.BC: Euripides Tragicus “Medea” 1036, etc.}}; γλυκύ [ἐστι], with _infinitive_, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|4th-5th c.BC: Aeschylus Tragicus “Prometheus Vinctus” 698, 2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 210; θανεῖν γλύκιστον 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 3.47; ὅτῳ.. μηδὲν ἦν ἰδεῖν γλυκύ 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Tyrannus” 1335 (Lyric poetry), compare 1390.}}
**b** of water, **sweet, fresh**, {{Refs 6th c.BC+|6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 1.8, etc.}}; opposed to πικρός, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Herodotus Historicus 4.52}}; opposed to ἁλμυρός, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Meteorologica” 355a33, etc.}}
**2** after {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Homerus Epicus (but see above)}}, of persons, **sweet, dear**, γλυκεῖα (variant{-ῆα}) μᾶτερ {{Refs 7th c.BC+|7th-6th c.BC: Sappho Lyrica 90; γλυκεῖαι παῖδες ἀρχαίου Σκότου 5th c.BC: Sophocles Tragicus “Oedipus Coloneus” 106}}: with _infinitive_, γ. φρὴν συμπόταισιν ὁμιλεῖν {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “P.” 6.52; frequently in epitaphs, “IG” 14.1472 (_superlative_)}}, etc.; also ὑπὲρ τῆς γλυκυτάτης πατρίδος τελευτῆσαι {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|“POxy.” 33i13 (2nd c.AD)}}; ὦ γλυκύτατε **my dear fellow**, {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Aristophanes Comicus “Acharnenses” 462, compare “Ec.” 124}}; sometimes in bad sense, **simple, silly**, ὡς γ. εἶ! {{Refs 5th c.BC+|5th-6th c.BC: Plato Philosophus “Hp.Ma.” 288b}}; also applied κατ᾽ ἀντίφρασιν to a swine, {{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD: Galenus Medicus 18(2).611}}; γλυκὺ πνεῖον, of mustard, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th c.BC: Matro Parodius “Convivium - Corpusculum poesis epicae ludibundae” 90.}}
**II** as _substantive_, ὁ γ. (i.e. οἶνος) **grape-syrup**, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|2nd c.AD: Alexander Rhetor 59, 172.14, 4th c.BC: Aristoteles Philosophus “Problemata” 875b2, 3rd c.BC: Herodas Mimographus 6.77, “POxy.” 1088.51; also τὸ γ. 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Alexipharmaca” 386, “POxy.” 234ii6 (2nd-3rd c.AD).}}
**II.b** of the eye of Polyphemus, {{Refs 3rd c.BC+|3rd c.BC: Theocritus Poeta Bucolicus 6.22.}}
**II.2** ἡ γ., ={γλυκύρριζα}, {{Refs 4th c.BC+|4th-3rd c.BC: Theophrastus Philosophus “Historia Plantarum” 9.13.2.}}
**II.3** ἡ γ., ={χολή}, {{Refs 2nd c.BC+|Scholia 2nd c.BC: Nicander Epicus “Theriaca” 594.}}
**III** _comparative_ and _superlative_ γλυκίων {{Refs 8th c.BC+|8th c.BC: Odyssea Homerus Epicus “Odyssey” 9.34; γλύκιστος 5th c.BC: Bacchylides Lyricus 3.47, 2nd-3rd c.AD: Aelianus “De Natura Animalium - Ar.Byzantine Epit.” 12.46, etc.; also γλυκύτερος, -τατος 5th c.BC: Pindarus Lyricus “O.” 1.109, 19, etc.; γλύσσων 6th c.BC: Xenophanes Poeta Philosophus 38.2.}}
**IV** _adverb_ **-κέως**{{Refs 2nd c.AD+|2nd c.AD(?): Pollianus Epigrammaticus 4.24. (Perh. from *δλυκύς}}, cf. Latin _dulcis._)
